\textbf{Defender's Strategy $\sigma^*$:}
The probability of allocating to $g^1$ is proportional to the progress towards $g^1$ that the \Mover{} has just made: i.e.,
\begin{equation} \label{eq: pi^E_A}
     \sigma_t^*(g^1| h_{t},e_t,s_t)=\sigma^*_t(g^1|c_{t-1},e_t)=\frac{c_{t-1}-c_t}{w(e_t)}\in[0,1]
\end{equation}
and $\sigma_t^*(g^2|c_{t-1},e_t)=1-\sigma_t^*(g^1|c_{t-1},e_t)$.\footnote{Note that $c_{t-1}$ is a sufficient statistic to implement $\sigma_t^*(g^i| h_{t},e_t,s_t)$ due to the Markovian property of $\sigma^*$. Also note that $\sigma^*$ is time invariant, and therefore we will drop the time subscript when appropriate.} 
