\section{Numerical Experiments}
\noindent
%This section presents a) illustrative examples to discuss the types of deceptive paths that emerge as PBNE solutions to our game in subsections~\ref{num sec. sec A} and~\ref{num sec. sec C}; b) comparative study between our PBNE deceptive and counter-deceptive strategies and deceptive policies derived from~\cite{Dragan2015DeceptiveRM} in subsections \ref{num sec. sec B},~\ref{num sec. sec C} and~\ref{num sec. sec D}.
To evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed strategies we use Value of Information defined below.
\begin{definition}[Value of Information (VoI).] We define VoI as the Defender's expected loss due to the lack of information with respect to the complete information value:
\begin{equation}
    \textnormal{VoI}(\sigma,\gamma;s_0,b_0) = \frac{\bar U(s_0,b_0) - U(\sigma,\gamma;s_0,b_0)}{\bar U(s_0,b_0)},
\end{equation}
    where $\bar U(s_0,b_0)\triangleq b_0^1 \cdot \bar U^1(s_0) + b_0^2 \cdot \bar U^2(s_0)$ is the complete information value over both games (see Remark~\ref{rem. Complete information game.}).
\end{definition}
%Note that neither player has a preference for either a high or low VoI, since VoI alone does not provide information about payoff. However given high and low VoI corresponding to the same initial conditions but different player strategies high VoI is preferred by the Attacker and low VoI is preferred by the Defender.
For the same $s_0$ and $b_0$, the optimal strategies satisfying~\eqref{eq:equilibrium} will also optimize the VoI, where the \Eater{}/\Mover{} is the minimizer/maximizer.
We use $\text{VoI}^*\triangleq\text{VoI}(\sigma^*,\gamma^*)$ to denote the equilibrium $\text{VoI}$.
Importantly, for different environments and/or different $(s_0,b_0)$, VoI can be used as a metric to quantify the effectiveness of deception in a particular scenario.
