The whole procedure of the proposed algorithm is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{algorithm:high_order}. 

We make several remarks about Algorithm~\ref{algorithm:high_order}. Although our algorithm requires looping over the indices $i$, $n$ and $t$, we note that once the value of $Y_T$ is fixed, the sequence $\{x_{\tau_{t, i}}^{(n)}\}_{0\leq i \leq K-1, 0\leq n \leq N, 1\leq t\leq T}$ is purely deterministic.
If we have perfect information on the true score function (at the requested points), the discrepancy between the distribution of $Y_t$ and $X_t$ only stems from interpolating a continuous function using polynomials of degree $K-1$. 

Our algorithm requires computing the score functions at the sequence $\{x_{\tau_{t, i}}^{(n)}\}_{0\leq i \leq K-1, 0\leq n \leq N, 1\leq t\leq T}$. The total number of score function evaluations is equal to $TKN$. As we shall see shortly in our theoretical development,  $K$ is taken to be a constant, whereas $N$ is typically chosen to be logarithmically large. As a result, the total number of score function evaluation is still $\widetilde{O}(T)$. 

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{HEROISM -- High-ordER Ode-based dIffusion SaMpler} \label{algorithm:high_order}
	\DontPrintSemicolon
	\textbf{input:} $\tau_{t,0} = \tau_{t+1, K-1} = 1 - \overline{\alpha}_t$ for all $1 \leq t \leq T-1$, and $\tau_{T, 0} = 1 - \overline{\alpha}_{T}$. 
    Define $\tau_{t, i}$ according to Eq.~\eqref{eqn:brahms-tau-i}.\\
	\textbf{initialization:} $t = T$, $ x_{\tau_{T, 0}} \leftarrow Y_{T}\sim \mathcal{N}(0, I_d)$.\\
	\For{$t > 1$} 
	         {$x_{\tau_{t, 0}} \leftarrow Y_t$ and $x_{\tau_{t, i}}^{(0)} \leftarrow Y_t$ for all $i = 0, \dots, K-1$\\
	         	\For {$n = 0, \dots, N-1$}
                {
                Update $(1-\tau)^{-3/2}s_{\tau}^{(n)}$ via Eqn.~\eqref{eq:score_update} for all $\tau \in [\tau_{t,K-1}, \tau_{t,0}]$\\
                Update $x_{\tau_{t,i}}^{(n+1)}$ via Eqn.~\eqref{eq:update_rule} for all $i = 0, \dots, K-1$
                }
                $Y_{t-1} \leftarrow x_{\tau_{t, K-1}}^{(N)}$\\
                $t \leftarrow t - 1$
	        }
    \textbf{output:} $Y_1$    
\end{algorithm}
