	\section{Spin Spacetime~Algebra}		  

	No bivector is found in the Dirac equation because his point-particle spin is defined in Minkowski space, a four-dimensional real manifold. In contrast, introducing a bivector gives spin structure and~since it can be oriented randomly relative to Minkowski space; we introduce spin spacetime, $\left( \beta_s,e_1,e_2,e_3 \right)$, which is the Body-Fixed (BFF) of one spin. Minkowski space is the Laboratory-Fixed Frame (LFF) $\left( \beta,X,Y,Z \right)$.  

	Dirac's gamma matrices, $\left( {{\gamma }^{0}},{{\gamma }^{1}},{{\gamma }^{2}},{{\gamma }^{3}} \right)$, represent the 4x4 Dirac field. Within~this field, there are two point-particle spins of $\frac{1}{2}$---each described by the three Pauli spin components and the identity ($I,\sigma_{X},\sigma_{Y}, \sigma_{Z})$---which belong to the SU(2) group; each is the mirror twin of the other: Dirac's matter--antimatter~pair. %MDPI: Please check that the intended meaning has been retained. ok

