\subsection{Omics Background}
The \textit{central dogma of gene expression} is the general process through which genetic information flows within a biological system \cite{Crick1958-je}. In short, it states ``DNA makes RNA, and RNA makes protein" \cite{Leavitt2010}. Understanding this process is crucial to furthering many fields within healthcare and life sciences, including medical research and diagnostics \cite{Liu2023}, hereditary studies \cite{Luo2018}, drug development \cite{SchneiderPoetsch2018} and evolutionary biology \cite{dupre2010human} \textcolor{blue}{[I think we should cite some more, including maybe some old school papers]}.

Understanding how a cell organizes itself to respond to its environment by expressing the correct genes at the correct time is critical to understanding how a cell (and eventually an organism) sets up the optimal cell state under healthy condition, and how the process goes awry in a disease state. To highlight a simplified example, the step ``DNA makes RNA'' is more formally known as \textit{transcription}, which is in and of itself a large field of research \textcolor{blue}{[cite a source?]}. An RNA polymerase (guided by a complex set of regulatory events) binds to a section of the DNA called the \textit{promoter}, which causes the promoter to act as a template off which the RNA strand can be built \textcolor{blue}{[cite sources, more info as footnotes or appendix]}. Although this sequence of events is well established at a high level, the complex outcomes of different transcriptional events can be very hard to predict. Beyond this one single step, there are a huge number of complex layers that regulate not just transcription, but the downstream step of converting RNA to protein (called translation), not to mention all the regulation that takes place within the cell to make such processes happen when and where they should in response to their environment. Suffices to say, the more data we collect on these processes, the more we realize how complicated they are, and the level of sophisticated comptuational tools that will be required to understand them.   \textcolor{blue}{[cite a source?]}.

\subsection{Gene Regulatory Networks}
\label{subsec:gene_reg_network}
A popular avenue that biologists take to study such questions is to model how the transcription of one gene affects the transcription of its neighboring genes by using \textit{gene regulatory networks} (GRNs). These models aim to describe the complex and dynamic interactions between genes, their products (RNAs, proteins) and other molecules (eg metabolites), a process which can help to potentially identify pathways that underlie biological processes both in homeostasis and diseases states. \cite{chen_identification_2022, zhang_discovering_2022}. Current methods to model GRNs are often pairwise correlation- and regression-based. This has the advantage of being computational efficient \textcolor{blue}{[cite something]} and has even demonstrated promising results \cite{Sciammas2011,Roy2019}. However, the correlations modelled are computed only between pairs of genes regardless of the expression values of other genes in the same biological system, making it prone to biases \textcolor{blue}{[cite something?]}, and hence does not always model the behavior of underlying interactions accurately.
% Numan will move this section up to this point to the appendix

In the main text, we describe a model for a \textit{quantum single-cell GRN} (qscGRN). Her we describe the results a bit more in detail. The authors worked with single cell RNA sequencing data from lymphoblastoid cell lines from two data sources \cite{Osorio2019,SoRelle2021}. Of this dataset, the authors chose 6 genes to include when modelling the qscGRN - IRF4, REL, PAX5, RELA, PRDM1 and AICDA in the NF-$\kappa$B signaling pathway. The correlations in accordance with previous findings included positive correlations between PAX5-AICDA, where it is known that PAX5 plays a role in the B-lineage-specific control of AICDA transcription \cite{Tunyaplin2004,ShapiroShelef2005}. The correlations in contradiction to previous findings include a negative correlation between IRF4 and AICDA, where we know that IRF4 is known to induce AICDA expression through an indirect mechanism in the NF-$\kappa$B signaling cascade\cite{Sciammas2006}. And the correlations not present in the baseline model include a negative correlation between PRDM1 and AICDA, where some studies indicate that PRDM1 could silence AICDA expression in a dose-dependent manner\cite{Nutt2011}.

\subsection{PSO}
\label{subsec:pso} 
One of the earlier methods used to cluster gene-expression datasets for understand co-expression of genes is called the \textit{particle swarm operator} (PSO) algorithm, originally put forward to model movements of animals \cite{Kennedy,Shi}. The algorithm models each organism as a point particle in a high-dimensional space where each particle has a position and velocity vector. In each iteration, the position and velocity of each particle is updated (i.e. the particle `moves') according to its previously best position and the global best position, which is determined by minimizing a chosen cost function \cite{Zhang2015}. Although this algorithm has shown some success in clustering gene-expression datasets \cite{Chen2014-qu}, its main disadvantage is that it is not guaranteed a global convergence \cite{10.5555/935867} and is prone to being trapped in local optima \cite{985692}.

In particular, clinical trials in neurology and psychiatry are time-intensive, expensive and subjective, and have lower success rates than other indications, with a likelihood of approval (LOA) from Phase I at approximately 8 \% and 6 \%, respectively, compared to 26 \% and 19 \% for hematology and infections diseases respectively \cite{thomas2016clinical}. Moreover, the probability of success for Phase I to approval is approximately 8 \% without selection biomarkers, compared to 25 \% with selection biomarkers. Hence, neuro-psychiatry is a unique area where biomarkers will potentially have the most impact by driving smaller, less expensive, and more successful trials.

Within neuro-psychiatry, pain represents a large unmet need with respect to the lack of reliable biomarkers. Pain is a multi-dimensional sensory, cognitive, emotional experience, whereby clinical evaluation relies on rating scales and questionnaires that are qualitative, time consuming, prone to bias and do not capture the full spectrum of the clinical condition. Moreover, chronic pain is arguably a gateway to the opioid epidemic, whereby suboptimal management leads to over-prescription and misuse of opioids. Hence, quantitative and physiological biomarkers are urgently needed for a comprehensive and reliable diagnosis and assessment of pain in healthcare and clinical trials \cite{davis2020discovery}.

\subsection{Quantum sensing and further quantum technologies}

Magnetoencephalography (MEG) is a functional neuroimaging technique that measures the magnetic fields produced by the brain's electric currents. MEG’s limitations are being overcome using newly developed quantum technology (a collaborative effort by the University of Nottingham and Wellcome Centre for Human Neuroimaging at University College London) via the development of optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs), quantum enabled magnetic field sensors that offer similar sensitivity to superconducting quantum interference device (SQUID)s but without the need for cryogenics. This technology has been recently commercialized by QuSpin and integrated into a working prototype MEG device. Because of the smaller size, OPMs can be mounted directly on the surface of a human head, increasing sensitivity and bringing the sensing component closer to the brain, this allowing for deeper and more accurate measurement of neural activity in the brain non-invasively \cite{coleman2021quantum}.

\subsection{Quantum sensing and computing brain function} 
Electroencephalography (EEG) has been analyzed using a model inspired by quantum mechanics \cite{popiel2021exploring}, whereby a mathematical formalism was applied to model EEG data, revealing a tendency for EEG to be localized in anterior regions of the brain during rest, and more uniformly distributed while engaged in a task. Moreover, values analogous to the Heisenberg uncertainty principle were shown, suggesting a common underlying architecture of human brain activity in resting and task conditions (constant KBrain) without claiming that the brain behaves as a quantum object.

Aside from the theoretical discourse about quantum computing in the brain championed by Hameroff and Penrose \cite{jibu1994quantum}, the possibility of neural processing using nuclear spins in the brain has been explored at the molecular level, with phosphorus (Posner molecule) identified as a biological substrate  with a nuclear spin that could possibly serve as a qubit for quantum processing, and one that could protect the neural qubits on long times and thereby serve as a quantum-memory \cite{fisher2015quantum, weingarten2016new}. More recently, however, the Posner molecule was shown to assume lower than previously thought symmetry structures at room temperature \cite{agarwal2021dynamical}.

If processing ``quantum data" is indeed somehow relevant to brain function at the macroscopic level, this would make quantum computers particularly suited to discovering new insights in the processing of brain-related data. This is because loading large volumes of ``classical data" remains a challenge for quantum computers while loading quantum data is a more natural fit \cite{aimeur2006machine, schuld2018supervised}. Thus, given that quantum sensors are already more mature in terms of commercialization than quantum computers \cite{bongs2023quantum}, biomedical areas where quantum sensors play key roles may end up being key areas for quantum computers to drive large impact.
