%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% Introduction %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}
In complex dynamics, iterated functions generate rich and intricate structures known as Julia sets. For a polynomial 
\( F: \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C} \), the \textbf{filled Julia set} is the set of points whose orbits under iteration of \( F \) remain bounded, while the \textbf{Julia set} is its boundary. 
Variants such as the \textbf{alternate Julia set}, which alternates between different polynomials at each step of iteration, reveal new fractal geometries and dynamical behaviors beyond the classical setting.

This paper introduces a new object of study: the \textbf{$p$-adic alternate Julia set}. Extending the concept of alternate Julia sets to the non-Archimedean setting of $\mathbb{C}_p$, the $p$-adic analog of the complex field $\mathbb{C}$, we define these sets analogously using the $p$-adic norm to assess orbital boundedness. The resulting dynamics exhibit features distinct from their complex counterparts, shaped by the topology of $\mathbb{C}_p$.

We begin by reviewing the classical theories and properties of filled Julia sets, Mandelbrot sets, and alternate Julia sets introduced in \cite[Section 2]{MR2567586}, along with a visualization algorithm and examples illustrating a range of connectivity properties of alternate Julia sets discussed in \cite[Sections 2 and 3]{MR3080737}. Building on this foundation, we review the existing theory of $p$-adic Mandelbrot and Julia sets before formulating a definition of $p$-adic alternate Julia sets by composing two given functions or dynamics in the $p$-adic setting. We then prove several foundational properties of these sets, highlighting the novel behaviors of alternate Julia sets in the $p$-adic context and drawing comparisons with the classical complex case. Specifically, the connectivity of a given $p$-adic alternate Julia set can be determined similarly to its complex counterpart—that is, by checking the boundedness of the conjugate $p$-adic Julia set generated by composing the two alternating dynamics. The patterns of some $p$-adic alternate Julia sets can also be determined by comparing the magnitude of the degree of the composed dynamics and the $p$-value, as described in \cite[Theorem 1]{MR3113229}.


