\begin{abstract}
% insert abstract here
	The hot phonon bottleneck effect is a promising mechanism for the realization of a true hot carrier solar cell.
	Prior work has assumed that the acoustic phonons created via decay of polar longitudinal optical (LO) phonons are assumed to quickly leave the system or thermalize quickly due to multi-phonon processes.
	The present work furthers the models of previous work to include a build-up of longitudinal acoustic phonons in addition to LO  phonons due via the Klemens process.
	By including this additional process, nonphysical assumptions concerning the LO anharmonic lifetime are no longer required, resulting in a better explanation of the experimental results, and pointing towards new approaches in achieving high carrier temperatures during photo-excitation. 
\end{abstract}

