\subsection{Physical noise} \label{sec:3b}

We now move on to discuss the effect of physical noise as introduced according to the procedure discussed in Sec.~\ref{physnoise}. We then initialize all fast variables $v_n$'s to zero and increase the noise strength $\Gamma$. This is illustrated in Fig. \ref{transition_n=1000}, where the percentage of solved cases for a single problem instance (with $N=1000$ variables)  with respect to the noise strength is plotted for clause-to-variable ratio $M/N=7$, along with the power spectrum of the fast variables' flow vector field $\textnormal{d}\mathbf{v}/\textnormal{d}t$ for representative instances. For each value of $\Gamma$, 100 realizations of the noise are averaged over. A problem is considered unsolved if it does not reach a solution within 10,000 steps. In order to isolate the effect of noise, all instances are initialized identically. This choice is justified in Fig. S6, which demonstrates that using random voltages initial conditions and instances results in the same power spectrum behavior as using fixed ones. To minimize numerical errors, following the results presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:1}, we have chosen an integration time step $\Delta t$ of 0.05. (However, in Sec.~\ref{adaptive}, we consider an adaptive time step to further control the numerical errors while increasing the problem size.) The main plot shows the sharp decline in the success rate as noise increases, indicating the solvable-to-unsolvable transition.

