\section{Discussion} \label{sec:4}


In this study, we employed various indicators to examine how numerical and physical noise impact the behavior of digital memcomputing machines. From the practical perspective, the influence of both types of noise appears similar: with increasing noise strength, the machines' problem-solving capability diminishes from 100\% to a lower percentage, eventually to 0\%. This is evident in Fig.~\ref{fig:1} for numerical noise and Fig.~\ref{transition_n=1000} for physical noise.

To analyze the loss of solvability, we employed the Lyapunov exponent analysis. We utilized the algorithm by Benettin {\it et al.}~\cite{benettin1980lyapunov} in the study of numerical noise, and a conventional algorithm to handle physical noise, leading to the determination of the largest Lyapunov exponents\footnote{We note that the Lyapunov exponent analysis was focused exclusively on the fast degrees of freedom (the logical variables). We believe that these are adequate for assessing whether the overall system dynamics (logical variables combined with memory variables) exhibit chaotic behavior.}. The averaged LLEs are presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:1} for numerical noise and in Fig.~\ref{transition_n=1000} for physical noise. Both scenarios exhibit a steady increase in the averaged LLEs; however, with numerical noise, this increase slightly precedes the decline in the solved instance percentage, whereas with physical noise a delay is observed. The reason for this observation is a possible underestimation of LLE by the standard algorithm that evaluates a single exponent versus the spectra in the algorithm by Benettin {\it et al.}~\cite{benettin1980lyapunov}.  Additionally, the standard algorithm was applied to a modified system (without constraints on the $v_n$'s) that may have a slightly different behavior. Based on the LE analysis, we have concluded that the solvable-unsolvable transition in digital memcomputing machines can be associated with a noise-induced chaos.


%As was stated above, chaos refers to the state of the systems that is characterized by being unpredictable. Despite the unpredictability, chaotic systems follow specific rules and are not inherently random. The main properties of chaos are sensitivity to initial conditions, topological mixing and dense periodic orbits ~\cite{strogatz2018nonlinear}. One of the key tools for identifying chaos used in most scientific studies is Lyapunov exponent analysis \cite{ispolatov2015chaos,nychka1992finding,albers2006persistent,musielak2009high,albers2006routes,blumenthal2024phase}. Following those works we calculated Lyapunov exponents of our systems and determined which of our dynamics is chaotic. It is also important to notice that due to the complexity of our system, for the Lyapunov exponent analysis we only observed the voltage variables and completely ignored the memory variables, which making our work different than studies that discuss chaos in the entire system.

The power spectra found in this work (see, e.g., Figs.~{\ref{fig:2}}-\ref{transition_n=1000}) provide additional insight into the dynamics of digital memcomputing machines.
In the case of numerical noise, we have noticed that all instances go through four stages when increasing the noise level: at small time steps, they exhibit either no chaos or weak ``transient chaos'' by showing very little or no chaotic behavior; as the time step increases, they enter the stage of ``transient chaos'' while still being able to reach the solution; and by increasing the time step further the system becomes fully chaotic and does not solve anymore. In the final stage, when $\Delta t$ is very large, the power spectra include spikes indicating a strong periodic component. It was observed that at very large time steps, the logical variables oscillate between $\pm 1$. In the case of physical noise, all stages but the last one are also present. This difference may be explained by the fact that unlike physical noise, numerical noise is ``non-local'' in time.


