\section{$1/f$ noise}\label{1/f_noise}
As mentioned in the main text, in the case of physical noise there is an additional feature present in the power spectra at very low frequencies (Inset (2) in Fig. \ref{transition_n=1000}). We attribute this feature to flicker noise~\cite{DuttaHorn}. This phenomenon is characterized by a power spectral density that scales inversely with frequency, following the form $1/f^{\alpha}$, with typically $1\leq\alpha\leq2$.

%While attempting to predict the white noise spectrum of "thermionic tube" amplifiers, J.B. Johnson observed an unexpected phenomenon known as "flicker noise" at low frequencies \cite{PhysRev.26.71}. W. Schottky found the origin based on the physics of electron transport in vacuum tubes. Flicker noise was then found to occur repeatedly across a wide range of systems (geology, biology, economics...) not only in electrical devices.

%Random walks and more general diffusion processes were identified as potential sources of 1/$f$ noise. For instance, Brownian motion exhibits a power spectrum proportional to 1/$f^2$. A 1/$f$ noise originates from two key factors intrinsic to any fluctuating observable measured in experiments: its initial conditions and its relaxation processes ~\cite{Diaz_2014}. Any stochastic process will have a 1/$f$ power spectral density down to the lowest measurable frequency $f$ whenever $f < \Omega_{min}$, where $\Omega_{min}$ is the minimal relaxation frequency. Therefore, introducing physical noise is expected to lead to the appearance of  $1/f$ noise at low frequencies,

\begin{figure}[tb]
\centering
    (a) \includegraphics[width=0.44\textwidth]{physicalnoise/fitlineairegamma_1.0.pdf}
