\begin{abstract}
We find necessary and sufficient conditions on weights
$u_1, u_2, v_1, v_2$, i.e.  measurable, positive, and finite, a.e. on $(a,b)$, for which there exists a positive constant $C$ such that for given $0 < p_1,q_1,p_2,q_2 <\infty$ the inequality
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
\bigg(\int_a^b \bigg(\int_a^t f(s)^{p_2} v_2(s)^{p_2} ds\bigg)^{\frac{q_2}{p_2}} u_2(t)^{q_2} dt \bigg)^{\frac{1}{q_2}}& \\ & \hspace{-3cm}\le 
C \bigg(\int_a^b \bigg(\int_a^t f(s)^{p_1} v_1(s)^{p_1} ds\bigg)^{\frac{q_1}{p_1}} u_1(t)^{q_1} dt \bigg)^{\frac{1}{q_1}}
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
holds for every non-negative, measurable function $f$ on $(a,b)$, where $0 \le a <b \le \infty$. The proof is based on a recently developed discretization method that enables us to overcome the restrictions of the earlier results.
\end{abstract}

