\section{Main results}
\label{sec:setup}

Consider a classical nonlinear dissipative system
whose state can be described by a vector of $N$ real variables
$X=(X_1,\ldots,X_N)$ and the dynamics is governed  by  a system of
stochastic nonlinear differential equations
\be
\label{SDE}
dX_i(t) = -\lambda_i X_i(t) dt  + b_i(X(t))dt  + \sqrt{q}\, dW_i(t),  \qquad t\ge 0
\ee
with a given  initial state $X(0)$.
Here $t$ is the evolution time, $\lambda_i>0$ are dissipation rates,
$b_i(x)$ are scalar drift functions,  
$q>0$ is the noise rate, and $W(t)\in \RR^N$ is 
a vector of $N$ independent Wiener processes (Brownian motions). In discrete time this system is related to the following random walk:
\[
X_i(t+dt) = X_i(t)  -\lambda_i X_i(t) dt + b_i(X(t)) dt + \sqrt{q dt } \calN(0,1),
\]
where $\calN(0,1)$ is the standard normally distributed random variable.
We consider the initial value problem where the goal is to approximate  the expected value of some designated 
time-evolved variable $X_i(t)$ with respect to Wiener noise
for a given initial state $X(0)$. Below we assume that the variables are ordered such that
\[
\lambda_1\le \lambda_2\le \ldots \le \lambda_N.
\]

It will be convenient to divide each drift function  into the  linear and nonlinear parts as
\be
b_i(x) = c_i(x) +\sum_{j=1}^N b_{i,j} x_j
\ee
where $b_{i,j}$ are real coefficients
 and $c_i\, : \, \RR^N\to \RR$ are arbitrary (nonlinear) functions which have
well-defined derivatives $\partial c_i(x)/\partial x_j$ for all $i,j$. 
The system is called {\em sparse} if there exists a constant $s=O(1)$
such that each drift function $c_i(x)$ depends on at most $s$ variables,
each row and each column  of the matrix $b=\{b_{i,j}\}$ has at most $s$ nonzeros, and the initial state $X(0)$ has at most $s$ nonzeros. 

We provide rigorous upper bounds on the runtime and simulation error for systems with a relatively bounded
nonlinearity, as formally described  in Section~\ref{sec:Kolmogorov}.
For example, any system satisfying
\be
\label{Jdefinition}
J=\sup_{x\in \RR^N} \left( \sum_{i=1}^N c_i^2(x)\right)^{1/2} <\infty
\ee
is  relatively bounded\footnote{We leave as an open question whether any system with a  relatively bounded nonlinearity has a finite $J$.}.
We shall refer to the parameter $J$ as the {\em nonlinear strength}.
The system has the {\em linear strength} $J_1$ if $|b_{i,j}|\le J_1$ for all indices $i,j$.


For general nonlinear systems the norm of the solution can become infinite after a finite evolution time
and the problem becomes ill-defined. To avoid this situation, we restrict our attention to a  class
of nonlinear systems satisfying certain zero divergence  condition.
To the best of our knowledge, this class was introduced by Engel, Smith, and Parker~\cite{engel2021linear} in the study of quantum algorithms based on linear embedding methods.
The system Eq.~(\ref{SDE}) is said to be {\em divergence-free} if the drift functions obey
\be
\label{NDE1}
\sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\partial }{\partial x_i} c_i(x)=0,
\ee
\be
\label{NDE2}
\sum_{i=1}^N \lambda_i x_i c_i(x)=0
\ee
for all  $x\in \RR^N$ and
\be
\label{NDE3}
\lambda_i b_{i,j} = -\lambda_j b_{j,i}
\ee
for all $1\le i,j\le N$. 
Note that Eq.~(\ref{NDE1}) is the standard divergence-free property of a vector field $c(x)$. The extra conditions Eqs.~(\ref{NDE2},\ref{NDE3}) ensure that the dynamics of the expected value of a function of the solution of Eq.~(\ref{SDE}) is described by a combination of unitary and dissipative time evolutions. For example, suppose all dissipation rates $\lambda_i$ are the same
and $c_i(x)=0$ for all $i$. Then condition Eq.~(\ref{NDE3}) is equivalent to skew-symmetry of the matrix $b=\{b_{i,j}\}$ and
the noiseless version of the system Eq.~(\ref{SDE}) has a solution $X(t) = e^{-\lambda_1 t} e^{bt} X(0)$.
Note that $e^{bt}$ is a unitary (in fact, orthogonal) matrix describing time evolution under a hermitian Hamiltonian $ib$, up to the damping factor $e^{-\lambda_1 t}$.
Examples of nonlinear  divergence-free systems are considered in Section~\ref{sec:examples}.


We are interested in the regime when the number of variables $N$ is large enough that
storing the full description of the system and its solution in a classical computer memory
is impractical. Accordingly, we  assume that
the system  admits a succinct description by classical 
algorithms with the runtime scaling polynomially with $\log{(N)}$
that compute dissipation rates $\lambda_i$,
 values and locations of nonzero
 matrix elements $b_{i,j}$, and certain coefficients of drift functions $c_i(x)$ expanded in the basis of Hermite polynomials,
 see Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} for details.


Our quantum algorithm outputs a single number that approximates the expected value of a  given scalar function $u_0 \, : \, \RR^N\to \RR$
evaluated at the solution $X(t)$. The function $u_0$ describes the property of the solution that we would like to observe. 
For example, $u_0(x)=x_i$ if we are  interested in the expected value of a single variable $X_i(t)$
or $u_0(x)=x_ix_j$ if we are interested in the expected value of $X_i(t) X_j(t)$. 
Let  
\begin{align}
    u(t,x) &= \EE_W u_0(X(t)|X(0)=x),
    \label{eq:utx}\\
    v(t,x)&=\EE_z u(t,x+z),\quad z_i\sim \calN(0,q/(2\lambda_i)).\label{eq:vtx}
\end{align} 
Here $u(t,x)$ is the expected value of $u_0(X(t))$ along the trajectory $X(t)$ starting from $X(0)=x$ with respect to Wiener noise $W$, and $v(t,x)$ is the expected value of $u(t,x+z)$ provided $z\in \RR^N$ is a Gaussian random vector with zero mean and variance $q/(2\lambda_i)$ for each variable $z_i$. 
% be the expected value of $u_0(X(t))$ with respect to Wiener noise $W$
% provided that $X(0)=x$. Let 
% $v(t,x)=\EE_z u(t,x+z)$,
% where $z\in \RR^N$ is a Gaussian random vector with zero mean
% and variance $q/(2\lambda_i)$ for each variable $z_i$. 
The random vector $z$ models initial state preparation errors. Note that the noise rate $q$ controls the strength of both Wiener noise and the initial state noise\footnote{We expect that our quantum algorithm remains efficient if Wiener noise and the initial state noise have different rates. We focus on the case of equal noise rates because this simplifies the analysis.}. 
Our main result is an end-to-end quantum algorithm that approximates the quantity $v(t,x)$ for any sparse divergence-free nonlinear system. This algorithm is visualized on Fig.~\ref{fig:diagram}. 
\begin{figure}
\centering\includegraphics[width=14cm]{diagram.pdf}
        \caption{
        Nonlinear  classical dynamics and its simulation on a quantum computer. The dynamics is divided into three stages describing initialization, time evolution, and the final measurement. {\em Classical dynamics:}
    the system  is initialized in a state $X(0)=x+z\in \RR^N$, where $x$ is the specified initial condition and $z$ is a Gaussian noise.
    Time evolution is governed by a system of  stochastic ordinary differential equations with a dissipation term $-\lambda_i X_i$, nonlinear
    drift term $c_i(X)$,  and Wiener noise. For simplicity we ignore the linear drift.
    The measurement step outputs the expected value of a specified
