\end{figure}
To state our result, define a weighted norm  $\|x\|_\lambda$ of a vector $x\in \RR^N$ such that
\be
\| x\|_\lambda^2  = \sum_{i=1}^N \lambda_i x_i^2.
\ee 
Below we assume that $u_0(x)=\prod_{i=1}^N x_i^{d_i}$ where $d_i\ge0$
are integers such that $\sum_{i=1}^N d_i=O(1)$. By linearity, it is straightforward to extend the algorithm to arbitrary
constant degree polynomials  $u_0(x)$.
\newpage
\begin{theorem} 
\label{thm:algorithm}
There exists a quantum algorithm taking as input a sparse initial state $X(0)\in \RR^N$, evolution time $t$,
an error tolerance $\epsilon$, and a polynomial $u_0(x)$ as above. 
The algorithm outputs a real number approximating the expected value of $u_0(X(t))$
with respect to both Wiener noise and the initial state noise with an additive error $\epsilon$. 
The algorithm has runtime scaling
polynomially with  $\log{(N)}$,  $t$, $\epsilon^{-1}$, $J$, $J_1$, $\lambda_1^{-1}$, and $\lambda_N$.
The runtime scales exponentially with the quantity $q^{-1} \|X(0)\|_\lambda^2$.
\end{theorem}
The exponential factor in the runtime is $\exp{\left(C q^{-1} \|X(0)\|_\lambda^2\right)}$,
where $C=O(1)$ is a universal constant. 
 Thus our algorithm is efficient only if the quantity $q^{-1} \|X(0)\|_\lambda^2$ is small enough. For example, suppose $X(0)$ is a single basis vector, that is,
$X_i(0)=\delta_{i,j}$ for some index $j$. Then $\|X(0)\|_\lambda^2 = \lambda_j$, and the 
runtime is proportional to $e^{C\lambda_j/q}$ times a polynomial function of $\log{(N)}$
and all other parameters. Note that the exponential factor can be made constant if $\lambda_j=O(q)$.

%\textcolor{red}{$\EE X_1(t)$ is changed to $v(t,x)$ below}
A natural question is whether the considered simulation problem can be solved by a classical algorithm with
a comparable or better runtime. Our second result shows that this  is highly unlikely since the problem of approximating 
the quantity $v(t,x)$ defined in~\eqref{eq:vtx} with the initial condition satisfying $q^{-1} \|X(0)\|_\lambda^2=O(1)$ is BQP-complete,
that is, as hard as simulating the universal quantum computer.
This is the case even if all drift functions are linear, that is, $c_i(x)=0$ for all $i$.
\begin{theorem} 
\label{thm:BQP}
Suppose $U$ is a quantum circuit on $n$ qubits of size $m=poly(n)$ such that each gate of $U$ acts on at most $k$ qubits
and has real matrix elements in the standard basis. 
There exists a divergence-free  classical system with $N=(m+1)2^n$ variables,  efficiently computable linear drift functions $b_i(x)$,
dissipation rate $\lambda_i=1/10$ for all $i$, noise rate $q=1/10$, 
 $s=2^{1+k}$, and $J_1=O(2^{1+k}m)$ such that 
\be
\label{U00}
\left|  \la 0^n|U|0^n\ra - v(t,x) \right|\le \frac1{10}
\ee
for $t=1$.
The initial state $X(0)$  is a basis vector, that is, $X_i(0)=\delta_{i,j}$
for some index $j$. The initial state obeys $q^{-1} \|X(0)\|_\lambda=1$.
\end{theorem}
Approximating the matrix element $\la 0^n|U|0^n\ra$ with a constant additive error, say  $1/3$, is the canonical example
of a BQP-complete problem~\cite{kitaev2002classical}. Theorem~\ref{thm:BQP} implies that approximating the quantity
$v(t,x)$ for $t=1$ is also BQP-complete even in the regime when $q^{-1} \|X(0)\|_\lambda^2=1$.
In this regime the runtime of our quantum algorithm is polynomial in all parameters.

Let us  emphasize that our main technical contribution is Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm} whereas Theorem~\ref{thm:BQP}
follows almost immediately from the definitions.  Indeed, since the considered Gaussian noise has 
zero mean and the system considered in Theorem~\ref{thm:BQP} is linear, noise does not contribute to the
observed expected value. Thus we just need to prove BQP-completeness for the noiseless version of the linearized system Eq.~(\ref{SDE})
which requires only a minor modification of the known circuit-to-Hamiltonian mapping~\cite{kitaev2002classical}.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:Kolmogorov} collects all necessary facts about the Kolmogorov equation, Gauss-Sobolev spaces, and 
Wiener-It\^{o} decomposition. Besides, in this section we identify special properties of the Kolmogorov equation associated with divergence-free systems.
Section~\ref{sec:readout} presents the first key ingredient of our quantum algorithm — an  efficient readout step that  extracts the desired expected value from Hermite expansion. 
Our second ingredient is the regularized Kolmogorov equation and a rigorous upper bound on the regularization error presented in Section~\ref{sec:regul-sz}.
This is the key for reducing an infinite dimensional simulation problem to a finite dimensional one with a well controlled approximation error.  
We combine the two ingredients to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm} in Section~\ref{sec:algorithm}.
Section~\ref{sec:examples} describes examples of divergence-free systems: an anharmonic oscillator and 
the incompressible \ac{nse} in two spacial dimensions on a torus. The latter are discretized in the eigen-basis of the Stokes operator~\cite{flandoli1998kolmogorov} to obtain a quadratic divergence-free system. Unlike the oscillator, this system has natural dissipation (molecular diffusion) and is stochastically forced. Both systems have infinite nonlinear strength ($J=\infty$). We numerically simulate our quantum algorithm
and compare its outputs with expected values simulated by the brute force sampling. For the case of \ac{nse} we use simple analytic solution (Taylor-Green vortex) to test our algorithm in the limit of very small noise without changing the dissipation.
Section~\ref{sec:BQP} contains the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:BQP}. We provide proofs of some technical results in the appendix.


