\section{Kolmogorov equation in Gauss-Sobolev space}
\label{sec:Kolmogorov}

In this section we describe our main technical tools: namely we show that $u(t,x)$ defined by~\eqref{eq:utx}, the expectation of a function of a solution of nonlinear SDE~\eqref{SDE}, solves a linear Partial Differential Equation (PDE), the Kolmogorov Equation (KE). Then we study KE's solvability in a coordinate Hilbert space, so called Gauss-Sobolev space. The latter is built upon Ito-Wiener decomposition, and allows one to map functions to vectors: namely, function $u$ is expanded into a Hermite polynomial series, and then it is uniquely mapped to a vector of coefficients, representing $u$ in the Hermite polynomial expansion. We provide a rather intuitive and less formal description of the embedding which applies to very regular $u(t,x)$ defined in~\eqref{eq:utx}. A more rigorous and formal derivation is available in~\cite{da2004kolmogorov}. %Also we note that well-posedness of SDEs of the type Eq.~(\ref{SDE}) was established earlier, see e.g. Theorem~1.1 of~\cite{liu2013well}.
%In particular, this theorem implies that $\EE  \sup_{t\in [0,T]} X_i^p(t)<\infty$ for all $T\ge 0$ and all integer $p\ge 0$.
%{\bf SZ: please check the reference above}

Given a vector $x\in \RR^N$ and  time $t\ge 0$, let $X(t)$ be a solution of Eq.~(\ref{SDE}) with the initial condition $X(0)=x$.
Note that $X(t)$ is a random variable since it depends on Wiener noise realization.
In this section we assume that the initial state $X(0)$ is noiseless.
Given a sufficiently regular test function  $u_0\, : \, \RR^N\to \RR$, let $u(t,x)$ be the expected value of $u_0(X(t))$ over Wiener noise, considered as a function
of the evolution time $t$ and the initial state $x=X(0)$. 
One can also write 
\be
\label{u(t)}
u(t,x)= \int_{\RR^N} dy \, \rho_t(x,y) u_0(y),
\ee
where  $\rho_t(x,y)$ is the probability density of a random variable $y=X(t)$ with the initial condition $x=X(0)$. 
By definition,
\be
u(0,x)=u_0(x)
\ee
for all $x\in \RR^N$. Define a normalized Gaussian probability distribution 
\be
\label{measure_mu}
\mu(x) \sim e^{-q^{-1} \|x\|_\lambda^2} = \exp{\left[ -\frac1q \sum_{i=1}^N \lambda_i x_i^2\right]}
\ee
such that $\int_{\RR^N} dx \mu(x)=1$. The   algorithm
claimed in Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm}
aims to approximate the quantity
\be
v(t,x) =  \int_{\RR^N} dz\,  \mu(z) u(t,x+z).
\ee
Here $z\in \RR^N$ represents the initial state error. 

Time evolution of the expected value $u(t,x)$ is governed by the backward Kolmogorov equation~\cite{da2004kolmogorov}. Assuming that $u(t,x)$ is a sufficiently smooth function, the classical Kolmogorov equation (KE) reads as
\be
\label{Kolmogorov1}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} u(t,x) = \sum_{i=1}^N (-\lambda_i x_i + c_i(x)) \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i} u(t,x)
+\frac{q}2 \sum_{i=1}^N  \frac{\partial^2}{\partial x_i^2} u(t,x).
\ee
Note that KE is different from the Fokker–Planck equation.
In the quantum physics language, KE describes time evolution of an observable embodied by the test function $u_0$,
while the Fokker–Planck equation describes time evolution of a state represented by the probability density function  $\rho_t(x,y)$. Solvability of KE~\eqref{Kolmogorov1} was studied by many authors, in particular for the case of $b_i$ being bounded and continuous together with its 1st and 2nd derivatives there is a unique classical solution $u$ of~\eqref{Kolmogorov1}, i.e. $u$ is continuously differentiable w.r.t. $t$ and $x$, and has bounded 2nd derivatives ~\cite{krylov1999kolmogorov}. In general, however KE lacks a classical solution and one needs to relax the notion of the solution as we will be discussing below. To prepare for this let us compactly re-write KE as 
\be
\label{Kolmogorov0}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} u(t,x)  = (-\calA +\calB+\calC) u(t,x), 
\ee
where
\be
\label{KolmogorovA}
\calA  = -\frac{q}2 \sum_{i=1}^N  \frac{\partial^2}{\partial x_i^2}  + \sum_{i=1}^N \lambda_i x_i \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i},
\ee
\be
\label{KolmogorovA1}
\calB= \sum_{i,j=1}^N b_{i,j} x_j \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}
\ee
\be
\label{KolmogorovB}
\calC= \sum_{i=1}^N c_i(x) \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}
\ee
Here $\calA$ describes~\footnote{$\calA$ is defined with a flipped sign for reasons which will become apparent in the next section.} the effect of  dissipation and noise, $\calB$ describes a linear drift,
and $\calC$ describes the nonlinear part of the drift. 

We formally rewrite KE in the  integral form
\be
\label{Kolmogorov2}
u(t,x) = e^{-t \calL} u_0(x) + \int_0^t ds\, e^{-(t-s)\calL} \calC u(s,x),\quad t\ge0,
\ee
where
\be
\calL = \calA-\calB.
\ee
Let us mention without going into mathematical details for now that the exponential $e^{-t\calL}$ denotes a solution operator of $\frac{\partial}{\partial t} u(t,x)  = (-\calA +\calB) u(t,x), $ it is a well-defined bounded linear operator which has a "smoothing" effect: in what follows we will see that $e^{-t\calL}\calC$ is a bounded linear operator in a certain Gauss-Sobolev space in contrast to $\calC$ which is unbounded differential operator. We will make this representation more precise below. %which : intuitively since $\calL$ is a direct sum of finite-dimensional blocks and we can exponentiate each block independently.
One can easily check that 
Eq.~(\ref{Kolmogorov2}) is equivalent to Eq.~(\ref{Kolmogorov0})
whenever all derivatives of $u(t,x)$ are well defined.
A function $u(t,x)$ solving Eq.~(\ref{Kolmogorov2}) is often called a {\em mild solution} of KE. To demonstrate that KE has the unique mild solution, and to study its properties, e.g. continuity and differentiability in space and time, we need to formally define multivariate Hermite polynomials and Gauss-Sobolev spaces~\cite{chow2007stochastic}.  


%We will see that operator $\calA$ is diagonal in the basis of Hermite polynomials as per discussion in the following section. 

\subsection{Hermite polynomials}
\label{sec:hermite}

Suppose $x\in \RR$ is a single variable.
We use probabilist's Hermite polynomials defined as
\be
\herm_n(x) = (-1)^n e^{x^2/2} \frac{d^n}{dx^n} e^{-x^2/2},
\ee
where $n\ge 0$ is the degree of $\herm_n(x)$. 
For example, $\herm_0(x)=1$, $\herm_1(x)=x$, $\herm_2(x)=x^2-1$, and
$\herm_3(x)=x^3-3x$.
It is well known that 
\be
\label{Hermite_equation}
\frac{d^2}{dx^2} \herm_n(x) - x \frac{d}{dx} \herm_n(x) = -n \herm_n(x)
\ee
for all $n\ge 0$. Let $\ZZ_{\ge 0}$ be the set of non-negative integers. 
Define a set of multi-indices 
\be
\calJ = \{ (m_1,m_2,\ldots,m_N) \in \ZZ_{\ge 0}^N  \, : \,  m_1+m_2+\ldots+m_N\ge 1\}.
\ee
For each multi-index $\mm \in \calJ$ define a polynomial
\be
\label{Hermite_normalized}
\mathbb{H}_{\multi{m}}(x) = \prod_{i=1}^N  \frac1{\sqrt{(m_i)!}} \herm_{m_i} \left(x_i\sqrt{2\lambda_i/q}\right),
\ee
where $x\in \RR^N$. The order of $\HH_\mm(x)$ is defined as
\be
|\mm|=\sum_{i=1}^N m_i.
\ee
Using the  orthogonality relations 
\be
\label{Hermite_nm}
\int_\RR dx\, e^{-x^2/2}   \herm_n(x) \herm_m(x) =(2\pi)^{1/2} n! \, \delta_{n,m}
\ee
and the chosen normalization of $\mathbb{H}_{\multi{m}}(x)$ one can check that 
\be
\label{H_inner_product}
\int_{\RR^N} dx \, \mu(x)\mathbb{H}_{\multi{m}}(x)  \mathbb{H}_{\multi{m'}}(x) = \delta_{\multi{m},\multi{m'}}
\ee
for all $\multi{m},\multi{m'}\in \calJ$, where $\mu(x)$ is the normalized Gaussian distribution,
\be
\mu(x)\sim \exp{\left(-q^{-1} \|x\|_\lambda^2\right)}.
\ee
\begin{lemma}\label{l:AB-HermiteBasis}
   $\calA$ is a diagonal operator in the basis of Hermite polynomials: 
   \be
\label{eigenvectors_of_L}
\calA\,  \mathbb{H}_{\multi{m}}(x) = \lambda_{\multi{m}}  \mathbb{H}_{\multi{m}}(x) 
\ee
for any multi-index $\mm\in\calJ$, 
where
\be
\label{lambda_m}
\lambda_{\multi{m}}  = \sum_{i=1}^N m_i \lambda_i.
\ee
Operator $\calB$
preserves the order of Hermite polynomials: 
\be
\label{KolmogorovL2explicit}
\calB\, \HH_\mm(x) =\sum_{i,j=1}^N \beta_{i,j} (m_i (m_j+1))^{1/2} \, \HH_{\mm - e^i + e^j}(x).
\ee
where $\beta_{i,j} = b_{i,j}\lambda_j^{-1/2} \lambda_i^{1/2}$ and 
$e^i\in \calJ$ is a multi-index with $e^i_i=1$ and $e^i_j=0$ for all $j\ne i$. 
\end{lemma}
Note that $\beta$ is a skew-symmetric matrix due to the divergence-free condition Eq.~(\ref{NDE3}).
The proof of the lemma is given in Appendix~\ref{sec:appB}. It implies, in particular that $\calB$ is a block-diagonal operator, where each block is spanned by Hermite polynomials
of a fixed order. Each block is a finite-dimensional matrix since the number of $N$-variate Hermite polynomials of a fixed order
is finite.

\subsection{Gauss Sobolev spaces}
\label{sec:GSpace}
