\subsection{
Coordinate Gauss-Sobolev space }
\label{sec:chaos}
As per Definition~\ref{dfn:solution} we will be looking for the solution of KE in the space $C(0,T,\calG\calS_0)$, the space of continuous functions with values in $\calG\calS_0$. As discussed above any such function $u$ can be written as 
\be
\label{chaos_eq1}
u(t,x) = \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{|\mm|=k, \mm\in \calJ} \psi_\mm(t) \HH_\mm(x) = \sum_{\mm\in \calJ} \psi_\mm(t) \HH_\mm(x) \in \calG\calS_0
\ee
for some real coefficients $\psi_\mm(t)$. The ansatz  Eq.~(\ref{chaos_eq1}) is inspired by the Wiener-It\^{o} decomposition of~$\calG\calS_0$, see e.g.~\cite{delgado2016spectral,da_prato_second_2002}. This ansatz suggests that every function\footnote{As noted above, a class of functions which are  almost everywhere (w.r.t. $\mu$) the same pointwise.} $u$ can be uniquely mapped to a vector of coefficients $\psi_\mm(t)$. In what follows we map KE into the coordinate space of coefficients.   

To describe time evolution of the coefficients $\psi_\mm(t)$ define a coordinate Hilbert space $\mathsf{GS}_k$ isomorphic 
to the Gauss-Sobolev space $\calG\calS_k$.
The isomorphism identifies each Hermite polynomial $\HH_\mm\in \calG\calS_k$ with a basis
vector $|\mm\ra\in \mathsf{GS}_k$. By definition, 
\be
\mathsf{GS}_k = \left\{ \sum_{\mm\in \calJ} \psi_\mm |\mm\ra \, : \,  \sum_{\mm\in \calJ} \lambda_\mm^p \psi_\mm^2 <\infty \quad
\mbox{for $p=0,1,\ldots,k$}\right\}.
\ee
The inner product on $\mathsf{GS}_k $ is defined such that $\la \nn|\mm\ra =\delta_{\nn,\mm}$. 
Let $\theta$ be an isomorphism that sends an operator $\calO$ 
acting on $\calG\calS_k$ to an operator $\theta(\calO)$ acting on $\mathsf{GS}_k$ such that 
\be
\la \nn |\theta(\calO)|\mm\ra  = \int_{\RR^N} dx\, \mu(x) \HH_\nn(x) \calO \HH_\mm(x).
\ee
Define operators
\be
A  = \theta(\calA), \quad B= \theta(\calB), \quad C = \theta(\calC).
\ee
Operators $A$, $B$ have domain $\mathsf{GS}_2$ and $C$ has domain $\mathsf{GS}_1$. %\textcolor{red}{I think we need to define $A$ on $\mathsf{GS}_1$, otherwise the relative boundedness condition does not make sense for all $\psi_1\in \mathsf{GS}_1$ - see below}
Let 
\be
\Lambda=A-B.
\ee
Now we formally substitute Eq.~(\ref{chaos_eq1}) into~\eqref{eq:KEmild}, and take the inner product (in $\calG\calS_0$) of the resulting equation with Hermite polynomial $\HH_{\mm}$. Using orthogonality~\eqref{H_inner_product} and Lemma~\ref{l:AB-HermiteBasis} it is not hard to establish that $u(t,x)$ defined in Eq.~(\ref{chaos_eq1}) is the solution of KE Eq.~(\ref{eq:KEmild}) with the initial condition $u(0,x)=u_0(x)\in \calG\calS_0$
iff the vector
of coefficients 
\be
|\psi(t)\ra = \sum_{\mm\in \calJ} \psi_\mm(t) |\mm\ra \in \mathsf{GS}_0
\ee
obeys
\be
\label{KE_initial}
\psi_\mm(0) =  \int_{\RR^N} dx\,\mu(x) \HH_\nn(x) u_0(x)
\ee
for all $\mm\in \calJ$ 
and
\be
\label{Kolmogorov5}
|\psi(t)\ra = e^{-t \Lambda } |\psi(0)\ra + \int_0^t ds \, e^{-(t-s)\Lambda} C |\psi(s)\ra.
\ee
If one additionally assumes that $|\psi(t)\ra$ is differentiable then Eq.~(\ref{Kolmogorov5}) is equivalent to 
\be
\frac{d}{dt} |\psi(t)\ra = (-\Lambda +C)|\psi(t)\ra.
\ee
The relative boundness condition Eq.~(\ref{gamma_definition}) is equivalent to 
\be
\label{gamma_eq1}
|\la \phi | C|\psi\ra | \le  \gamma\sqrt{\la \phi|\phi\ra \cdot \la \psi|A |\psi\ra}
\ee
for all $\psi \in \mathsf{GS}_1$ and $\phi \in \mathsf{GS}_0$. %\textcolor{red}{I think we need to define $A$ on $\mathsf{GS}_1$ - the weak form rather than in a strong form: your $A=\theta(\calA)$ is a map of a Laplacian $\calA$, which is a strong form, but it should be a map of a square root of Laplacian, $\calA^\frac12$ the weak form. For a strong $A$ the relative boundedness condition does not make sense for all $\psi_1\in \mathsf{GS}_1$, to make it sound $\la \psi|\Lambda|\psi\ra$ should be $\sum \lambda_{\mm}\psi_{\mm}^2$ but if $A=\theta(\calA)$ is defined the way you did above we have $\la \psi|\Lambda|\psi\ra = \sum \lambda^2_{\mm}\psi_{\mm}^2$. }

\textbf{Remark.} Operators acting on $\mathsf{GS}_k$ can be conveniently described using the quantum second quantization formalism
for a system of $N$ bosons (quantum harmonic oscillators). 
 Let $a_1,\ldots,a_N$ be the bosonic annihilation operators with domain $\mathsf{GS}_1$ and range $\mathsf{GS}_0$
 such that 
\[
a_i |\mm\ra = \left\{
\ba{rcl} 
\sqrt{m_i} |\mm-e^i\ra &\mbox{if} & m_i\ge 1,\\
0 &\mbox{if} & m_i=0\\
\ea
\right.
\]
for all $\mm\in \calJ$.
Accordingly, $a_i^\dag |\mm\ra = \sqrt{m_i+1} |\mm+e^i\ra$ and
\[
a_i^\dag a_i |\mm\ra = m_i |\mm\ra.
\]
Note that the order of Hermite polynomials corresponds to the particle number. 
The derivative operator $f(x)\to \partial f(x)/\partial x_i$ 
and the multiplication operator $f(x)\to x_i f(x)$ 
are represented by the
operators $(2\lambda_i/q)^{1/2} a_i$ and $(2\lambda_i/q)^{-1/2}(a_i+a_i^\dag)$ in the second quantization.
From Lemma~\ref{l:AB-HermiteBasis} one gets 
\be
\label{A_second_quantized}
A = \sum_{\mm \in \calJ} \lambda_\mm |\mm\ra\la \mm| =  \sum_{i=1}^N \lambda_i a_i^\dag a_i
\ee
and
\be
\label{B_second_quantized}
B= \sum_{i,j=1}^N \beta_{i,j} \, a_j^\dag a_i.
\ee
Recall that  the matrix 
$\beta_{i,j} = b_{i,j}\lambda_j^{-1/2} \lambda_i^{1/2}$ is skew-symmetric for divergence-free systems. 
Hence $B$ is a skew-hermitian operator,
\be
B^\dag = -B.
\ee
From Lemma~\ref{lemma:Cprops} one also gets
\be
C^\dag = -C.
\ee
Suppose $|\psi(t)\ra$ is differentiable, Then 
\be
\label{norm_derivative}
\frac{d}{dt}\la \psi(t)|\psi(t)\ra = 2\la \psi(t)| (-\Lambda+C)|\psi(t)\ra = - 2\la \psi(t)|A|\psi(t)\ra \le 0.
\ee
Indeed, $B^\dag = -B$ and $C^\dag=-C$ imply $\la \psi|B|\psi\ra=0$
and $\la \psi|C|\psi\ra=0$ for all $\psi \in \mathsf{GS}_1$.
Furthermore, $\la \psi|A|\psi\ra\ge 0$ for all $\psi \in \mathsf{GS}_1$.
Thus the norm of $|\psi(t)\ra$ is a monotone non-increasing function of time. As we show below,
the same conclusion holds even if 
$|\psi(t)\ra$ is not differentiable.


\subsection{Regularized Kolmogorov equation}
\label{sec:regul-sz}

In this section we prove that KE in the form~\eqref{Kolmogorov5} has the unique solution, and we propose a constructive regularization procedure which approximates the solution of KE and bounds the approximation error. %up to which the regularized KE approximates the bare (unregularized) one. 
Choose regularization parameters
\(
0<r\le R<\infty
\)
and partition the set of all multi-indices $\calJ$ into two disjoint subsets
\be
\label{J1J2}
\calJ = \calJ_1 \cup \calJ_2
\ee
such that the following conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\calJ_1$ is a finite set.
\item $\lambda_\mm \le R$ for all $\mm \in \calJ_1$,
\item $\lambda_\mm\ge r$ for all $\mm \in \calJ_2$,
\item $\la \nn |B|\mm\ra  = 0$ for all $\nn\in \calJ_1$ and $\mm \in \calJ_2$,
\end{enumerate}
Note that all multi-indices with $\lambda_\mm<r$ are in $\calJ_1$, all multi-indices with $\lambda_\mm>R$ are in $\calJ_2$,
and the remaining multi-indices with $r\le \lambda_\mm \le R$ can be placed in either set, as long as condition~(4) is satisfied. 
We shall refer to the tuple $(r,R,\calJ_1,\calJ_2)$ satisfying the above conditions as a regularization scheme.

Loosely speaking, our quantum algorithm simulates KE projected onto subspace of basis vectors corresponding to $\calJ_1$. The parameter $r$ controls the regularization error, that is, the discrepancy between solutions of the projected and the exact  KE  (larger $r$ means smaller error) while $R$
controls the  norm of a Hamiltonian associated with the regularized  KE
(larger $R$ means higher-norm Hamiltonian). 
Unless the system has a special structure, one can use two general-purpose regularization schemes defined  below.

First,  choose an arbitrary $r>0$ and let $R= r(\lambda_N/\lambda_1)$.  Let
\be
\label{partition_1}
\calJ_1=\{ \mm \in \calJ \, : \, |\mm| \le r/\lambda_1\} \quad \mbox{and} \quad \calJ_2=\calJ \setminus \calJ_1.
\ee
Let us verify the four conditions above.  Condition~(1) is satisfied by obvious reasons. 
If $\mm \in \calJ_1$ then
$\lambda_\mm \le  |\mm| \lambda_N\le r(\lambda_N/\lambda_1) = R$. If $\mm\in \calJ_2$ then $|\mm|>r/\lambda_1$ and thus
$\lambda_\mm \ge \lambda_1 |\mm| > r$.
Condition~(4) is satisfied bceause $B$ preserves the number of particles $|\mm|$.

Second, suppose  $b_{i,j}=0$ for all $i,j$. Then $B=0$ and condition (4) is satisfied automatically.
In this case one can choose $r=R$, 
\be
\label{partition_2}
\calJ_1=\{ \mm \in \calJ \, : \,  \lambda_\mm \le r\} \quad \mbox{and} \quad \calJ_2=\calJ \setminus \calJ_1.
\ee
The set $\calJ_1$  is finite since $|\mm|\le \lambda_\mm/\lambda_1\le r/\lambda_1$ for all $\mm\in \calJ_1$.
Conditions (2) and (3) hold by obvious reasons. 

For each $i=1,2$ define a projector
\be
\Pi_i = \sum_{\mm \in \calJ_i} |\mm\ra\la \mm|.
\ee
Clearly, $\Pi_i$ defines a linear operator on the Hilbert space $\mathsf{GS}_k$ for any $k\ge 0$
and $\Pi_1+\Pi_2=I$.
Let us write
\be
C = C_{11} + C_{12} + C_{21} + C_{22},
\ee
where
\be
C_{ij} = \Pi_i C \Pi_j.
\ee
Since $C$ is skew-hermitian, one has
\be
C_{11}^\dag = -C_{11}, \quad C_{22}^\dag = -C_{22}, \quad C_{21}^\dag = -C_{12}.
\ee
Define the regularized KE as 
\be\label{eq:KEreg}
|\phi(t)\ra = e^{-t\Lambda }|\phi(0)\ra  + \int_0^t ds\, e^{-(t-s)\Lambda} C_{11} |\phi(s)\ra,
\ee
with the initial condition $|\phi(0)\ra = |\psi(0)\ra$.
Obviously, time evolution of the projection $\Pi_2 |\phi(t)\ra$ is completely decoupled from $\Pi_1|\phi(t)\ra$.
We can replace the integral KE by the differential KE in the block $\Pi_2$
since $-\Lambda$ generates Ornstein–Uhlenbeck semigroup and the block $\Pi_2$
does not get any contribution from $C$.
We can replace the integral KE by the differential KE in the block $\Pi_1$ since this block is finite dimensional.
Hence the projected KE is equivalent to 
\be
\label{regul_eq1}
\frac{d}{dt} |\phi(t)\ra = (-\Lambda + C_{11})|\phi(t)\ra.
\ee
%This assumption will be relaxed  later.
The following theorem demonstrates that the parameter $r$ controls the regularization error. %$\| \psi(t) - \phi(t) \|^2=\la\psi(t)-\phi(t)|\psi(t)-\phi(t)\ra$. 
\begin{theorem}[\bf Regularization error]
\label{thm:regul}
Let $\phi(\cdot)$ denote the unique solution of~\eqref{regul_eq1} and assume that $\psi(\cdot)$ is the unique differentiable solution of Eq.~(\ref{Kolmogorov5}). Then Eq.~(\ref{Kolmogorov5}) is equivalent to 
\be
\label{regul_eq2}
\frac{d}{dt} |\psi(t)\ra = (-\Lambda + C)|\psi(t)\ra
\ee
and for all $t\ge 0$ one has
\be
\label{regul_error}
\| \psi(t) - \phi(t) \|^2 \le \frac{3\gamma^2}{2r} \|\psi(0)\|^2.
\ee
\end{theorem}
We defer the proof to Appendix~\ref{sec:appD}.
Let us now employ the above theorem to prove that KE~(\ref{Kolmogorov5}) has the unique solution $|\psi(t)\ra$ which might not be differentiable. 
%Then Eq.~(\ref{regul_eq2}) is not well-defined and  the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:regul}  requires a few amendments.
Consider a sequence of regularized KEs corresponding to a monotone increasing sequence of the regularization parameters  $r_k$ such that $r_k <r_{k+1}$ for all $k$ and $\lim_{k\to \infty} r_k=\infty$. Let $\calJ=\calJ_1^k \cup \calJ_2^k$ be the partition defined in Eq.~(\ref{partition_1}) with $r$ replaced by $r_k$.
Let $\Pi_i^k = \sum_{\mm \in \calJ_i^k} |\mm\ra\la \mm|$, where $i=1,2$. By definition,
$\calJ_1^k \subseteq \calJ_1^\ell$ and $\Pi_1^k \Pi_1^\ell = \Pi_1^k$ whenever $k<\ell$.
\begin{theorem}\label{t:existence}
 Let $|\phi_k(t)\ra\in \mathsf{GS}_1$ be the solution of the $k$-th regularized KE
\be
\frac{d}{dt} |\phi_k(t)\ra = (-\Lambda + \Pi_1^k C \Pi_1^k)|\phi_k(t)\ra,\quad |\phi_k(0)\ra = |\psi(0)\ra.
\ee
Then sequence of vector-valued functions $\{\phi_k(\cdot)\}_k$ converges (in the norm of $C(0,T,\mathsf{GS}_0)$) to a function $\psi(\cdot)$: 
\be
\label{regul_eq32}
\max_{t\in[0,T]}
\| \psi(t) - \phi_k(t) \|^2 \le \frac{3\gamma^2}{2r_k} \|\psi(0)\|^2
\ee
and $|\psi(t)\ra$ solves KE~(\ref{Kolmogorov5}) (in the sense of Definition~\ref{dfn:solution}): 
\[
|\psi(t)\ra =e^{-\Lambda t}|\psi_0\ra+\int_0^t ds\, e^{-\Lambda (t-s)} C |\psi(s)\ra,\quad t\ge0
\]
\end{theorem}

