\section{Readout state}
\label{sec:readout}

Here we discuss a number of fundamental mathematical questions related to the design of the readout protocol for our quantum algorithm. The actual design is then reported in 
Section~\ref{sec:readout_solution}.


\subsection{Mathematical challenges}
\label{sec:readout_challenges}

As we saw in section~\ref{sec:GSpace} above, the solution of KE, $u$ is a continuous function of time $t$ with values\footnote{It is also possible to show that $\int_0^T \|u(t)\|^2_{\mu,1} ds<+\infty$ so that for almost all $t$ $u(t)\in\calG\calS_1$.} in $\calG\calS_0$. Moreover, as noted in Section~\ref{sec:GSpace} such functions admit Hermite polynomial expansion which however is not necessarily converging pointwise. 
%For this reason, for a generic functions $u$ of this class, for a fixed $t>0$ the value $u(t,x)$ might not be well defined for every $x$ but rather it is defined almost everywhere w.r.t. Gaussian measure $\mu(x)dx=\frac{\lambda^{\frac N2}}{(2\pi q)^{\frac N2}}e^{-\|x\|^2\frac{\lambda}{2q}}dx$ (assuming that $\lambda_i=\lambda$). 
Naively one could try and design a readout protocol for quantum algorithm by using the form of the Hermite expansion of $u$: namely, since $u(t,x) = \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{|\mm|=k} \psi_\mm(t) \HH_\mm(x)$ then it seems obvious that $u(t,x)$ is equal to the inner product $\la\psi_{out}(x)|\psi(t)\ra$, provided $\mm$-th component of the vector $\psi_{out}(x)$ is given by $(\psi_{out}(x))_\mm=\HH_\mm(x)$: in other words, the vector\footnote{Recall the definition of isomorphism $\theta$ from Section~\ref{sec:chaos}} $\psi_{out}(x)=c(x) \theta(\delta(x-\cdot))$, where $\delta(x-\cdot)$ is the standard Dirac delta function concentrated at $x$, i.e. $\int_{\RR^N}dz\mu(z)\HH_\mm(z)\delta(x-z)=\mu(x)\HH_\mm(x)$, and $c(x)=1/\mu(x)$ is the normalization constant, so that 
\[
(\psi_{out}(x))_\mm = (c(x)\delta(x-\cdot),\HH_\mm)_\mu=c(x) \int_{\RR^N}dz\mu(z)\HH_\mm(z)\delta(x-z) = \HH_\mm(x)
\] 
and hence formally \[
\la\psi_{out}(x)|\psi(t)\ra = \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{|\mm|=k} \psi_{out}(x)_\mm \psi_\mm(t) = \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{|\mm|=k} \psi_\mm(t) \HH_\mm(x) = u(t,x)
\]
as desired. However, Dirac delta function $c \delta(x-\cdot)\not\in\calG\calS_0$ as $\|\delta(x-\cdot)\|_\mu=+\infty$. For the convenience of the reader we will provide a short demonstration of this fact below (see Section~\ref{sec:dirac}). 

Instead, we note that (for simplicity assuming that $\lambda_i=\lambda$) \[
\mu(x)dx=\frac{\lambda^{\frac N2}}{(2\pi q)^{\frac N2}}e^{-\|x\|^2\frac{\lambda}{2q}}dx
\] 
is an approximation of the Dirac delta: indeed for 
\[
v(t,x) =\int_{\RR^N}dy\,  \mu(x-y) u(t,y) = \int_{\RR^N}dy\,  \mu(y) u(t,x+y)
\]
it can be shown that $v(t,x)\to u(t,x)$ provided $\frac{q}{\lambda}\to0$ and $u$ is a continuous function of both $t$ and $x$. Importantly, $\mu(x-\cdot)\in\calG\calS_0$. Hence it has finite norm and is the best candidate for the readout state $\psi_{out}(x)$. Besides, the approximation of $u(t,x)$ by $v(t,x)$ has a natural probabilistic interpretation: $v(t,x)$ is an average of a function $u(t,\cdot)$ of the initial condition of the original nonlinear dynamical system, $X(0)=x$ which is a random variable with Gaussian density $\mu$.

\subsection{Our solution}
\label{sec:readout_solution}

 Here we show that for any $x\in \RR^N$ there exists a state
$|\psi_{out}(x)\ra\in \mathsf{GS}_0$ such that the desired expected value
$v(t,x)$ can be expressed as the inner product
\be
\label{readout_eq1}
v(t,x) = \la \psi_{out}(x)|\psi(t)\ra.
\ee
Here $|\psi(t)\ra \in \mathsf{GS}_0$ is the solution of KE.
We shall refer to $|\psi_{out}(x)\ra$ as the {\em readout state}.
Indeed, substituting the Hermite expansion of $u(t,x)$, see Eq.~(\ref{chaos_eq1}), gives
\[
v(t,x)=
\int_{\RR^N}dy\,  \mu(y) u(t,x+y)
=\sum_{\mm\in \calJ} \psi_\mm(t) \int_{\RR^N}dy\,  \mu(y)  \HH_\mm(x+y).
\]
Umbral identity Eq.~(\ref{umbral_eq1}) and orthogonality relations  Eq.~(\ref{Hermite_nm})  
for Hermite polynomials imply
\[
\int_\RR dy \, e^{-y^2/2} \herm_n(x+y)  = \int_\RR dy\,  e^{-y^2/2} \herm_n(x+y)   \herm_0(y) = (2\pi)^{1/2} x^n
\]
for any $x\in \RR$.
As a consequence,
\[
\int_{\RR^N} dy \, \mu(y) \HH_\mm(x+y) = \prod_{i=1}^N \frac1{\sqrt{(m_i !)}} \left( x_i \sqrt{\frac{2 \lambda_i}{q}}\right)^{m_i}
\]
for any $x\in \RR^N$. We arrive at
\be
v(t,x) = \sum_{\mm\in \calJ} \psi_\mm(t)  \prod_{i=1}^N \frac1{\sqrt{(m_i !)}} \left( x_i \sqrt{\frac{2 \lambda_i}{q}}\right)^{m_i} = \la \psi_{out}(x)|\psi(t)\ra,
\ee
where
\be
\label{psi_out}
|\psi_{out}(x)\ra = \sum_{\mm \in \calJ}
|\mm\ra \cdot  \prod_{i=1}^N \frac1{\sqrt{(m_i !)}} \left( x_i \sqrt{\frac{2 \lambda_i}{q}}\right)^{m_i}.
\ee
We note that $|\psi_{out}(x)\ra$ coincides with  the coherent state embedding of $x$
discussed in~\cite{engel2021linear}. 
The state $|\psi_{out}(x)\ra$ belongs to the Gauss Sobolev space $\mathsf{GS}_0$ since 
\be
\label{readout_state_norm}
\|\psi_{out}(x)\|^2 = \sum_{\mm\in \calJ}  \frac1{(m_i !)} \left( 2 q^{-1}     \lambda_i x_i^2 \right)^{m_i} =
\exp{\left[ 2q^{-1} \|x\|_\lambda^2\right]} < \infty.
\ee
Here we noted that $\psi_{out}(x)$ is a tensor product of states associated with each variable $x_i$ and the norm is multiplicative under the tensor product.


Our strategy realized in the next section is to estimate the inner product  $\la \psi_{out}(x)|\psi(t)\ra$
with a small additive error on a quantum computer. We shall see that normalized versions of the states $|\psi_{out}(x)\ra$ and $|\psi(t)\ra$ can be
well approximated by quantum circuits. 




\section{Quantum algorithm}
\label{sec:algorithm}

Our quantum algorithm simulates the regularized Kolmogorov equation (RKE)  of Section~\ref{sec:regul-sz} projected onto the subspace
\be
\calH_1=\mathrm{span}(|\mm\ra\, : \, \mm \in \calJ_1).
\ee
By definition, RKE does not couple $\calH_1$ with its orthogonal complement. Thus the solution of RKE never leaves $\calH_1$
provided that the initial state at time $t=0$ belongs to $\calH_1$. 
Below we always assume this is the case, that is, $|\psi(0)\ra \in \calH_1$. 
The number of qubits needed to encode $\calH_1$ and a specific encoding of multi-indices by bit strings
may depend on details of the regularization scheme. 
Below we use a general-purpose encoding proposed in~\cite{engel2021linear,tanaka2023polynomial}.
Namely, let 
\be
K=\max_{\mm \in \calJ_1} |\mm|
\ee
be the maximum order of Hermite polynomials in $\calJ_1$.
A  multi-index $\mm \in \calJ_1$ can be considered as a multiset\footnote{Recall that a multiset is
a set that may contain multiple copies of each element. The cardinality of a multiset is 
the sum of the multiplicities of all its elements.}
 $M \subseteq \{1,2,\ldots,N\}$
of cardinality $|M|\le K$ that contains $m_i$ copies of an integer $i$,   that is,
\[
M = \{ \underbrace{1,1,\ldots,1}_{m_1},  \underbrace{2,2,\ldots,2}_{m_2}, \ldots,  \underbrace{N,N,\ldots,N}_{m_N}\}.
\]
Define a tuple $(M_1,\ldots,M_K)$ such that 
$M_j$ is the $j$-th largest  element of  $M$ 
if $1\le j\le |M|$, and $M_j=0$ if $|M|<j\le K$. 
As a concrete example, suppose $N=7$ and $K=4$. Consider a multi-index
$\mm = (2,0,0,0,0,0,1)$. The corresponding multiset $M=\{1,1,7\}$
has cardinality $|M|=3$. Thus 
$M_1=7$, 
$M_2=M_3=1$, and $M_4=0$. The tuple $(M_1,\ldots,M_k)$ uniquely identifies $\mm$ since
$m_i$ is equal to the number of times an integer $i$ appears in $(M_1,\ldots,M_k)$.
Using the binary encoding of integers
one can identify $M_j$ with a bit string of length $q$, 
where  $q$ is the smallest integer such that $2^q \ge N+1$.
Then $(M_1,\ldots,M_k)$ is a bit string of length $qK$
that uniquely identifies $\mm$.  The total number of qubits used to express $\calH_1$ is
\be
\label{qubit_count}
Q = qK \approx K \log_2{(N+1)}.
\ee
In the above example $N=7$ and thus $q=3$.
A multi-index $\mm = (2,0,0,0,0,0,1)$  is encoded by a qubit basis state
\[
|111\ra \otimes |100\ra \otimes |100\ra \otimes |000\ra
\]
where $K=4$ registers store the binary encodings of  $M_1=7$, $M_2=M_3=1$, and $M_4=0$
(the least significant bit is the leftmost). 

The regularization schemes Eqs.~(\ref{partition_1},\ref{partition_2}) have  $K\le \lceil r/\lambda_1 \rceil$, where $r$ is the regularization parameter
yet to be specified. 
Recall that the solution of RKE approximates the solution of KE within error $O(r^{-1})$, see Theorems~\ref{thm:regul},\ref{t:existence}.
Therefore  the number of qubits one needs to encode $\calH_1$  scales linearly  with the inverse regularization error
and logarithmically with the number of variables $N$.

The required number of qubits can be reduced if the sequence $\{\lambda_i\}_i$ is rapidly growing.
In this case the preferred regularization scheme would be  Eq.~(\ref{partition_2}) with
$\calJ_1 = \{ \mm \in \calJ\, : \, \lambda_\mm \le r\}$.
Suppose $\mm \in \calJ_1$. Then $m_i=0$ for all $i$ such that $\lambda_i>r$. Hence we can use the above encoding of multi-indices by bit strings
with $Q\approx K \log_2{(N_r+1)}$ qubits, where $N_r$ is the number of indices $i\in [1,N]$ with $\lambda_i\le r$, which
can be  much smaller than $N$ if $\{\lambda_i\}_i$ is rapidly growing



Below  we describe main steps of the quantum algorithm claimed in Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm}:
initialization,  simulating  the time evolution associated with RKE, and an approximate preparation of the readout state.
We then combine all these steps to estimate the desired expected value $v(t,x)$ and derive an upper bound on the qubit and gate count. 
Our algorithm closely follows~\cite{engel2021linear,tanaka2023polynomial}
except for the readout step which appears to be new. 

\subsection{Initialization}
\label{sec:init}

Suppose our goal is to estimate the expected value of $u_0(X(t))$
for a polynomial  function
\be
u_0(x)=\prod_{i=1}^N x_i^{d_i},
\ee
where $d_i\ge 0$ and $|\dd|=\sum_{i=1}^N d_i=O(1)$. 
Let 
\be
\overline{u}_0 = \int_{\RR^N} dx \, \mu(x) u_0(x)
\ee
be the mean value of $u_0(x)$. 
The initial state of RKE (which is the same as the initial state of KE) is 
\be
|\phi(0)\ra =|\psi(0)\ra =  \sum_{\mm \in \calJ} \phi_\mm  |\mm\ra,
\ee
where $\phi_\mm$ are coefficients from the Wiener-It\^{o} decomposition 
\be
u_0(x)-\overline{u}_0 = \sum_{\mm \in \calJ} \phi_\mm \HH_\mm(x).
\ee
The norm of $|\phi(0)\ra$ can be computed analytically using Wick's theorem. It gives
\be
\label{phi(0)_norm}
\la \phi(0)|\phi(0)\ra = \int_{\RR^N} dx\, \mu(x) (u_0 - \overline{u}_0)^2(x)= \prod_{i=1}^N \frac{(2d_i)!}{2^{d_i} d_i !} (q/2\lambda_i)^{d_i} -\overline{u}_0^2,
\ee
where $\overline{u}_0=0$ unless $d_i$ is even for all $i$, in which case
\be
u_0 =  \prod_{i=1}^N \frac{(d_i)!}{2^{d_i/2} (d_i/2) !} (q/2\lambda_i)^{d_i/2}.
\ee 
By assumption, dissipation rates $\lambda_i$ are computable in time $poly(\log{(N)})$. Hence the norm of $|\phi(0)\ra$ is also computable
in time $poly(\log{(N)})$. 
Applying the well-known identity
\be
x_i^n = n! \sum_{0\le m\le n/2} \frac1{2^m m! (n-2m)!} \herm_{n-2m}(x_i)
\ee 
to each variable of $x$ 
shows that $|\phi(0)\ra$ is a superposition of $O(d^d)=O(1)$ basis vectors $|\mm\ra$  with efficiently computable coefficients. 
It is known~\cite{mao2024toward} that any $Q$-qubit state which is a superposition of at most $\chi$ basis vectors 
can be prepared by a quantum circuit of size linear in $Q + Q \chi/\log{(Q)}$.
Hence in our case the initialization cost is at most linear in the number of qubits $Q$, which is given by Eq.~(\ref{qubit_count}).
Below we implicitly assume that multi-indices $\mm\in \calJ_1$  are encoded by $Q$-bit strings. 
Let us consider simple examples. 

\noindent
{\em Example 1:}
Suppose our goal is to estimate the expected value of  $X_i(t)$.
Then $u_0(x)=x_i$ and $\overline{u}_0=0$.
The expansion of $u_0(x)$ in the basis of Hermite polynomials is
$u_0(x)= \sqrt{q/2\lambda_i} \HH_\mm(x)$, where $\mm=e^i$ is the multi-index with $m_i=1$ and $m_j=0$ for $j\ne i$.
The  initial state is 
$|\phi(0)\ra = \sqrt{q/(2\lambda_i)} |\mm\ra$. 


\noindent
{\em Example 2:}
Suppose our goal is to estimate the expected value of $X_i^2(t)$.
Then $u_0(x) = x_i^2$ and $\overline{u}_0 = q/2\lambda_i$.  Let $\mm=2e^i$ be a multi-index such that 
$m_i=2$ and $m_j=0$ for $j\ne i$.
To expand $u_0(x)-\overline{u}_0$ in the basis of Hermite polynomials note that $\herm_2(x)=x^2-1$ which gives
\[
\HH_{\mm}(x) = \frac1{\sqrt{2}} \herm_2( x_i \sqrt{2\lambda_i/q}) = \sqrt{2}\lambda_i q^{-1} x_i^2 - 1/\sqrt{2}.
\]
Hence $u_0(x) - \overline{u}_0 =  (q/\sqrt{2} \lambda_i)\HH_{\mm}(x)$ and the initial state is
$|\phi(0)\ra =  (q/\sqrt{2} \lambda_i) |\mm\ra$.



\subsection{Time evolution}
\label{sec:time_evolution}

Let $A_1$ and $B_1$ be the projections of $A$ and $B$ onto the subspace $\calH_1$.
Then the solution of RKE is
\be
\label{RKE_eq2}
|\phi(t)\ra = e^{t(-A_1 + B_1 + C_{11})} |\phi(0)\ra.
\ee
The following lemmas provide upper bounds on the norm and the sparsity of 
the operators $B_1$ and $C_{11}$ acting on $\calH_1$.
This determines the cost of simulating the time evolution $e^{t B_1}$ and $e^{t C_{11}}$ by the sparse Hamiltonian simulation 
methods~\cite{berry2014exponential,low2017optimal,low2017quantum,low2019hamiltonian}.
Note that $e^{t B_1}$ and $e^{t C_{11}}$ are unitary since we have already showed that $B$ and $C$ are skew-hermitian. 
Below  $R$ and $r$ are the regularization parameter introduced in Section~\ref{sec:regul-sz}.
Recall that $\lambda_\mm \le R$ for all $\mm \in \calJ_1$ and $\lambda_\mm \ge r$ for all $\mm \notin \calJ_1$.
Specific values of $r$ and $R$ will be chosen in Section~\ref{sec:runtime}. 
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:sparsityC}
Suppose each drift function $c_i(x)$ acts on at most $s$ variables. 
Then the matrix of $C_{11}$ in the standard basis has at most 
$K(K+1)^s$
nonzeros in every row and every column. Furthermore,
\be
\label{C11_norm}
\|C_{11}\|\le
\gamma R^{1/2},
\ee
where $\gamma = J\sqrt{q/2}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider a fixed column of $C$ labeled by a multi-index  $\mm \in \calJ_1$.
Let $\mathrm{supp}(\mm)$ be the set of all indices $i\in \{1,2,\ldots,N\}$ such that $m_i\ne 0$.
By definition, 
\[
 \la \nn|C|\mm\ra =  \sum_{i=1}^N  \int_{\RR^N} dx\,  \mu(x) \HH_{\multi{n}}(x)  c_i(x) \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i} \HH_{\multi{m}}(x)
\]
for any $\nn \in \calJ_1$. The zero order Hermite polynomial  $\herm_0(x)$ is a constant function so that 
$\partial/\partial x_i  \HH_{\multi{m}}(x)=0$ whenever $i\notin \mathrm{supp}(\mm)$.
If $m_i\ge 1$ then
 $\frac{\partial}{\partial x_i} \HH_{\mm}(x) =(2m_i \lambda_i/q)^{1/2}\, \HH_{\mm-e^i}(x)$.
Thus
\be
\label{C_matrix_element_eq1}
 \la \nn|C|\mm\ra = 
  \sum_{i \in \mathrm{supp}(\mm)}  (2m_i \lambda_i/q)^{1/2}
\int_{\RR^N} dx\,  \mu(x) \HH_{\multi{n}}(x)  c_i(x) \HH_{\mm-e^i}(x)
\ee
The orthogonality relations Eq.~(\ref{H_inner_product}) imply that $\int_{\RR^N} dx\,  \mu(x) \HH_{\multi{n}}(x)  c_i(x) \HH_{\mm-e^i}(x)=0$
unless $n_j=m_j$ for all indices $j$ such that the variable $x_j$ does not appear in $c_i(x)$.
Hence,  each index $i \in \mathrm{supp}(\mm)$ can create at most $(K+1)^s$ 
nonzero matrix elements in the $\mm$-th column of $C_{11}$. 
Furthermore, $|\mathrm{supp}(\mm)|\le K$, proving the claim for the sparsity of columns.
Since $C$ is a skew-symmetric, rows and columns have the same sparsity.

The relative boundness property Eq.~(\ref{gamma_eq1}) and condition (2) give
\be
\| C_{11} \| \le \| C \Pi_1 \| \le \gamma \| A^{1/2} \Pi_1\|= \gamma \max_{\mm \in \calJ_1} \lambda_\mm^{1/2} \le \gamma R^{1/2}.
\ee
\end{proof}

Recall that the drift functions in Eq.~(\ref{SDE})  have the form $b_i(x)= c_i(x)+\sum_{j=1}^N b_{i,j} x_j$, where
the matrix $b$ has at most $s$ nonzeros in each row and each column, and $|b_{i,j}|\le J_1$ for all $i,j$.
\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:sparsityB}
The matrix of $B_1$ in  the standard basis has at most 
$sK(K+1)$
nonzeros in every row and every column. Furthermore,
\be
\label{B1_norm}
\| B_1\| \le  s J_1 K(\lambda_N/\lambda_1)^{1/2}.
\ee
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider a fixed column of $B_1$ labelled by a multi-index  $\mm \in \calJ_1$.
For any $\nn \in \calJ_1$ one has
\be
\la \nn|B_1|\mm\ra = \sum_{i,j=1}^N \beta_{i,j} \la \nn|a_j^\dag a_i|\mm\ra.
\ee
Clearly, $ \la \nn|a_j^\dag a_i|\mm\ra=0$ unless $m_i\ge 1$. Thus the sum over $i$ contains at most
$|\mm|\le K$ nonzero terms. For each $i$ the sum over $j$ contains at most $s$ nonzero terms since $\beta$ is $s$-sparse.
Each pair $(i,j)$ can create at most $K+1$ nonzero entries in the column $\nn$ of $B_1$ since $n_j\in [0,K]$ and all other
indices of $\nn$ are fixed by the choice of  $\mm,i,j$. Thus the considered column of $B$ has at most $sK(K+1)$ nonzeros.
Since $B$ is skew-hermitian, rows and columns of $B_1$ have the same sparsity.
 This proves the first claim.

Since $B$ is skew-hermitian, 
$iB$ is a hermitian Hamiltonian describing a system of free bosons. It is well known that such 
Hamiltonians can be diagonalized by the
Bogolyubov transformation. Eigenvalues of $iB$  have the form $\pm \sum_{i=1}^N m_i \sigma_i$, where $\sigma_i$ are singular values of the matrix $\beta$ 
and $\mm\in \calJ$.  Since the Bogolyubov transformation preserves the number of particles, 
the  largest magnitude of eigenvalues of $iB$ restricted to the $K$-particle subspace is $K\max_i \sigma_i = K\|\beta\|$. 
By assumption,  the matrix $b$ is $s$-sparse and $|b_{i,j}|\le J_1$ for all $i,j$.
It follows that the matrix $\beta$ is also $s$-sparse and $|\beta_{i,j}|\le J_1 (\lambda_N/\lambda_1)^{1/2}$.
Gershgorin circle theorem then implies $\|\beta\| \le s J_1 (\lambda_N/\lambda_1)^{1/2}$
which proves the second claim.
\end{proof}
Finally, since $A_1$ is a diagonal matrix with entries $\la \mm|A_1|\mm\ra = \lambda_\mm$ for $\mm \in \calJ_1$, the norm
of $A_1$ is 
\be
\|A_1\| = \max_{\mm \in \calJ_1} \lambda_\mm \le R.
\ee
For simplicity, we simulate RKE by  Trotterizing the time evolution.
This is enough to get
 a polynomial time algorithm claimed in Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm}.
We expect that the polynomial degree can be optimized  using 
 more sophisticated quantum ODE solvers~\cite{krovi2023improved,an2023linear}.
Consider the first-order product formula 
\be
\label{product_formula}
\calS(t) = e^{-tA_1 } e^{tB_1} e^{ tC_{11}}
\ee
approximating $e^{t(-A_1 + B_1 + C_{11})}$ for a short evolution time $t$. 
Note that $\calS(t)$ preserves the subspace $\calH_1$.
Divide the time interval $[0,t]$ into $\ell$ Trotter steps
of length $t/\ell$.
Using  well-known upper bounds on the Trotter error, see e.g. Lemma~1 of~\cite{childs2021theory}, 
one gets
\be
\label{trotter_error}
\| \calS(t/\ell)^\ell |\phi(0)\ra -  |\phi(t)\ra  \| \le  O(t^2/\ell) (\| A_1\| + \|B_1\| + \|C_{11}\|)^2 e^{(t/\ell) (\| A_1\| + \|B_1\| + \| C_{11}\|)}  \|\phi(0)\|
\ee
for any evolution time $t\ge 0$ and any number of Trotter steps $\ell$. 
Choose the number of Trotter steps $\ell$ large enough that 
\be
(t/\ell) (\| A_1\| + \|B_1\| + \| C_{11}\|)=O(1).
\ee 
Substituting $\|A_1\|\le R$, $\|B_1\|\le J_1K (\lambda_N/\lambda_1)^{1/2}$, and $\| C_{11}\| \le \gamma R^{1/2}$
from Lemmas~\ref{lemma:sparsityC},\ref{lemma:sparsityB} into
Eq.~(\ref{trotter_error}) gives
\be
\label{Trotter_error_final}
\|   \calS(t/\ell)^\ell|\phi(0)\ra -  |\phi(t)\ra  \| \le O( t^2/\ell) (R^2 +J_1^2K^2 (\lambda_N/\lambda_1) +  \gamma^2 R  ) \|\phi(0)\|.
\ee


Let $\tau \equiv  t/\ell$. We need quantum circuits approximating the time evolutions  $e^{\tau B_1}$, $e^{\tau C_{11}}$, and $e^{-\tau A_1}$.
Recall  that $B_1$ and $C_{11}$ are skew-hermitian, that is, $e^{\tau B_1}$ and $e^{\tau C_{11}}$
are unitary. 
A single Trotter step $e^{\tau B_1}$ or $e^{\tau C_{11}}$ 
can be implemented using any available quantum algorithm for simulating unitary time evolution under a sparse Hamiltonian, see e.g.~\cite{berry2014exponential,low2017optimal,low2017quantum,low2019hamiltonian,haah2019product}.
These methods have cost scaling linearly with the evolution time, Hamiltonian norm, sparsity,
and logarithmically with the desired error tolerance. Here cost is measured by the required number of gates
and queries to the oracles computing matrix elements of the Hamiltonian, as discussed in details below.

Meanwhile, the operator $e^{-\tau A_1}$ is not unitary since eigenvalues of $A_1$ are real positive numbers. 
One can implement $e^{-\tau A_1}$ by a quantum circuit with a post-selective measurement.
 To this end  introduce one ancillary qubit such that the full Hilbert space is $\calH_1 \otimes \CC_2 \subseteq (\CC^2)^{\otimes Q+1}$.
Let $U$ be the controlled  rotation of the ancilla qubit defined as
\be
\label{postselection_gadget}
U|\mm\ra \otimes |0\ra = |\mm\ra \otimes (c_\mm  |0\ra + s_\mm |1\ra)
\ee
for all $\mm\in \calJ_1$, where $c_\mm = e^{-\tau \lambda_\mm}$ and $s_\mm = \sqrt{1-c_\mm^2}$.
The action of $U$ on states $|\mm\ra \otimes |1\ra$ is irrelevant. 
Consider an arbitrary state $|\psi\ra \in \calH_1\subseteq (\CC^2)^{\otimes Q}$.
Applying $U$ to  $|\psi\ra \otimes |0\ra$, measuring the ancillary qubit, and post-selecting the outcome $0$
implements the desired time evolution $|\psi\ra \to e^{-\tau A_1}|\psi\ra$.
The probability of observing the outcome $0$ is $P_0 = \| e^{-\tau A_1} \psi\|^2/\|\psi\|^2$.
By assumption, we are given a classical circuit of size  $poly(\log{(N)})$ that implements the map $i\to \lambda_i$.
Then the map $\mm\to \lambda_\mm\to c_\mm$ with $\mm \in \calJ_1$ can be implemented by a classical circuit of size $poly(K,\log{(N)})$.
It is straightforward to convert this classical circuit 
to  a quantum circuit of size $poly(K,\log{(N)})$
that implements the controlled rotation in Eq.~(\ref{postselection_gadget}). 
This may require $poly(K,\log{(N)})$ ancillary qubits to make all classical operations reversible. 
The  circuit $\calS(t/\ell)^\ell$  with $\ell$ Trotter steps contains $\ell$ occurrences of  $e^{-\tau A_1}$. The probability of observing the outcome $0$ in all $\ell$
Trotter steps is 
\be
P_{0^\ell} = \frac{\| \calS(t/\ell)^\ell \phi(0)\|^2}{\|\phi(0)\|^2}.
\ee

Finally, let us comment on the implementation of oracles required for sparse Hamiltonian simulation methods.
Below we mostly follow~\cite{low2017optimal,low2017quantum}.
Suppose $H$ is a Hermitian operator acting on $Q$ qubits such that any matrix element of $H$
can be specified exactly with $Q'$ binary digits. 
In our case $H\approx iB_1$ or $H\approx iC_{11}$, where the approximation indicates that we round all matrix elements
of $B_1$ or $C_{11}$ 
to $Q'$ binary digits. 
 Define an oracle $O_H$  as a unitary operator acting on $2Q+Q'$ qubits
such that 
\be
O_H |\nn\ra \otimes |\mm\ra \otimes |z\ra = |\nn\ra\otimes |\mm\ra \otimes |z\oplus H_{\nn,\mm}\ra,
\ee
for any $\nn,\mm\in \calJ_1 \subseteq \{0,1\}^Q$ and any $z\in \{0,1\}^{Q'}$, 
where $H_{\nn,\mm}$ is the binary encoding of $\la \nn|H|\mm\ra$ and $\oplus$ is the XOR of binary strings. 
The oracle is never queried on bit strings $\nn,\mm$ that do not encode multi-indices from $\calJ_1$. The action of $O_H$ on such 
inputs is irrelevant. 
Suppose each column  of $H$ has at most $s$ nonzeros.
Define an oracle $O_F$ as unitary operator acting on $2Q$ qubits such that 
\be
O_F |\mm\ra \otimes |j\ra = |\mm\ra \otimes |f(\mm,j)\ra,
\ee
where $\mm \in \calJ_1\subseteq \{0,1\}^Q$ and
$f(\mm,j)\in \{0,1\}^Q$ is the row index of the $j$-th nonzero entry in the column $\mm$ of $H$. 
Here it is assumed that $j\in \{1,2,\ldots,s\}$. 
Let 
\[
H_{max}=\max_{\nn,\mm\in \calJ_1}  |\la \nn | H|\mm\ra  |\le \|H\|.
\]
As was shown in~\cite{low2017optimal}, for any evolution time $\tau\ge 0$ and error tolerance $\epsilon>0$ there exists a quantum 
circuit $U$ acting on $Q$ data qubits and roughly $Q + Q'\log{(Q')}$ ancillary qubits such that 
$U$ approximates $e^{-i\tau H}$ within error $\epsilon$ on the subspace where all ancillary  qubits are initialized in $|0\ra$
and $U$ makes $O(\tau s \|H\|_{max} + \log{(1/\epsilon)})$ queries to $O_H$
and $O_F$. The circuit $U$ also includes a comparable number of single-qubit and  two-qubit gates. 
Suppose $H$ satisfies $\|H-iC_{11}\|_{max} \le 2^{-Q'}$.
Then $\| H - iC_{11}\| \le s2^{-Q'}$. This implies $\| e^{-i\tau H} - e^{\tau C_{11}}\| \le s \tau 2^{-Q'}$ and thus
$U$ approximates $e^{\tau C_{11}}$ within error  $\epsilon + s\tau 2^{-Q'}=O(\epsilon)$
provided that 
\be
\label{Q'}
2^{Q'}= \lceil s \tau \epsilon^{-1} \rceil = 1+ O(\tau \epsilon^{-1}).
\ee
 Similar bounds apply to
 $e^{\tau B_1}$. Replacing $\epsilon$ by $\epsilon/\ell$ and combining $\ell$ Trotter steps one gets a unitary quantum circuit $U_\ell$ 
with the query and gate complexity $O(t(\|C_{11}\| + \|B_1\|)) + O(\log{(\ell/\epsilon)})$ such that 
\be
\label{U_ell}
\| \calS(t/\ell)^\ell  - \la 0_{anc} |U_\ell |0_{anc}\ra \| \le \epsilon,
\ee
where $|0_{anc}\ra$ is the state of all ancillary qubits. 

The next step is to account the cost of implementing the required oracles. 
Suppose $H\approx i C_{11}$. 
A classical algorithm implementing the map $(\mm,j)\to f(\mm,j)$ can be readily extracted from the  proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:sparsityC}.
Indeed, let $\mathrm{supp}(c_i)$ be the subset of variables that appear in the function $c_i(x)$.
By assumption, $|\mathrm{supp}(c_i)|\le s=O(1)$.
We have established that $\la \nn|C_{11}|\mm\ra \ne 0$ 
only if there exist $i\in \mathrm{supp}(\mm)$ such that $\nn$ differs from $\mm$ only on coordinates
in $\mathrm{supp}(c_i)$, see the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:sparsityC}.
For a given $i \in \mathrm{supp}(\mm)$
there are at most $(K+1)^s$ choices of $\nn$ as above. 
A naive implementation of the oracle simply enumerates
all such pairs $(i,\nn)$, computes the corresponding list of multi-indices $\nn$,
sorts the list,  and 
returns the $j$-th multi-index $\nn$. Converting this algorithm  to a classical reversible circuit gives the desired oracle $O_F$.
It has gate complexity $poly(K,\log{(N)})$. To implement the oracle $O_H$ we need a classical algorithm
for the map $(\nn,\mm)\to H_{\nn,\mm} \approx \la \nn|C|\mm\ra$, where the matrix element is computed with $Q'$ binary digitis of precision. 
We have already established that 
\[
 \la \nn|C|\mm\ra = 
  \sum_{i \in \mathrm{supp}(\mm)}  (2m_i \lambda_i/q)^{1/2}
  C_{\nn,\mm,i} \quad \mbox{where} \quad
   C_{\nn,\mm,i}=
\int_{\RR^N} dx\,  \mu(x) \HH_{\multi{n}}(x)  c_i(x) \HH_{\mm-e^i}(x),
\]
see the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:sparsityC}.
Note that  $C_{\nn,\mm,i}=0$  unless $\nn$ and $\mm$
coincide outside the support of $c_i(x)$. Suppose  this is the case. 
Orthogonality of Hermite polynomials then implies that the integral 
$C_{\nn,\mm,i}$ can be
restricted to at most $s=O(1)$ variables in the support of $c_i(x)$. 
We assume that a classical algorithm is available that takes as input $i\in \{1,2,\ldots,N\}$,
multi-indices $\nn,\mm\in \calJ_1$, an error tolerance $\delta$, and outputs a real number
$C'_{\nn,\mm,i}$ such that $|C_{\nn,\mm,i}-C'_{\nn,\mm,i}|\le \delta$.
The algorithm should have runtime  $poly(K,\log{(N)},\delta^{-1})$.
Then 
\[
H_{\nn,\mm} \equiv   \sum_{i \in \mathrm{supp}(\mm)}  (2m_i \lambda_i/q)^{1/2}C_{\nn,\mm,i}'
\]
approximates $\la \nn|C|\mm\ra$ with an additive error 
\[
\delta' =O(\delta\lambda_N^{1/2} q^{-1/2}  |\mathrm{supp}(\mm)|  \max_i m_i)=O(\delta  K^2 \lambda_N^{1/2} q^{-1/2}).
\]
Since we need $Q'$ binary digits of precision, set $\delta'=2^{-Q'}$. Then  the classical algorithm implementing
the map $(\nn,\mm)\to H_{\nn,\mm}$ has runtime $poly(2^{Q'}, \log{(N)},K, \lambda_N, q^{-1})=poly(\tau,\epsilon^{-1},\log{(N)},K,\lambda_N,q^{-1})$,
which is polynomial in all relevant parameters. Here we used Eq.~(\ref{Q'}). Finally, the classical algorithm is converted to a quantum circuit
implementing the oracle $O_H$ by making all operations reversible. 
The oracles $O_F$ and $O_H$ associated with $B_1$ are implemented similarly using Lemma~\ref{lemma:sparsityB}.

To conclude, a unitary quantum circuit $U_\ell$ satisfying Eq.~(\ref{U_ell}) has gate complexity polynomial in all parameters
$\ell,t,\epsilon^{-1}, \log{(N)}, K, \lambda_N, q^{-1},J_1,R$.


\subsection{Readout state preparation}
\label{sec:readout_state_prep}

Let $|\psi_{out}(x)\ra$ be the readout state 
defined in Eq.~(\ref{psi_out})
and $\mathrm{supp}(x)\subseteq \{1,2,\ldots,N\}$ be the set of indices $i$ such that $x_i\ne 0$. 
By assumption, $|\mathrm{supp}(x)|\le s=O(1)$.
From Eq.~(\ref{psi_out}) one gets
\be
|\psi_{out}(x)\ra = \bigotimes_{i\in \mathrm{supp}(x)} \; |\varphi_i(x_i)\ra  \bigotimes_{i\notin \mathrm{supp}(x)} \; |0\ra
\ee
with
\be
|\varphi_i(x_i)\ra =  \sum_{m\ge 0}
 \frac1{\sqrt{(m!)}} \left( x_i\sqrt{\frac{2 \lambda_i}{q}}\right)^{m}  |m\ra.
\ee
Note that $|\varphi_i(x_i)\ra$ is the coherent state of a single boson. 

One can efficiently prepare a good approximation of $\varphi_{i}(x_i)$ by truncating the sum over $m$ at some finite order $k$.
Indeed, define a state
\[
|\varphi_{i}^{(k)}(x_i)\ra =  \sum_{m= 0}^k
 \frac1{\sqrt{(m!)}} \left( x_i \sqrt{\frac{2 \lambda_i}{q}}\right)^{m}  |m\ra.
\]
Using the bound 
\[
\left| e^x - \sum_{m=0}^k \frac{x^m}{m!}\right|\le \epsilon \quad \mbox{for} \quad
k\ge \frac{4x}{\log{(2)}} + \frac{\log{(1/\epsilon)}}{\log{(2)}}
\]
which holds for any $x\ge0$ and $\epsilon>0$, see Lemma~14 in~\cite{bravyi2021complexity},
one can ensure that 
\[
\| \varphi_{i}(x_i) -\varphi_{i}^{(k)}(x_i)\| \le \delta
\]
by choosing the cutoff
\[
k= \max_i \frac{8 \lambda_i x_i^2}{q \log{(2)}} +   \frac{2\log{(1/\delta)}}{\log{(2)}}.
\]
Our approximation of the readout state is 
\[
|\phi_{out}(x)\ra =  \bigotimes_{i\in \mathrm{supp}(x)} \; |\varphi_i^{(k)}(x_i)\ra  \bigotimes_{i\notin \mathrm{supp}(x)} \; |0\ra.
\]
From the triangle inequality,  the bound $|\mathrm{supp}(x)|\le s$, and $\| \varphi_i^{(k)}(x_i)\| \le \| \varphi_i(x_i)\|$  one gets
\be
\|  \psi_{out}(x)  -  \phi_{out}(x)\|  \le \delta s \| \psi_{out}(x)\|
\ee
Setting  $\delta= \epsilon/s \| \psi_{out}(x)\|$ gives 
\be
\|  \psi_{out}(x)  -  \phi_{out}(x)\|  \le \epsilon
\ee
provided that 
\[
k = \max_i \frac{8 \lambda_i x_i^2}{q \log{(2)}} +  O(\log{(\epsilon^{-1}   \| \psi_{out}(x)\|)}) = O(q^{-1} \|x\|_\lambda^2)+O(\log{(\epsilon^{-1})}).
\]
for all $i\in \mathrm{supp}(x)$.
Let $q\ge 1$ be the smallest integer such that $k+1\le 2^q$.
 Then the state $|\phi_i^{(k)}(x_i)\ra$ can be embedded into a 
$q$-qubit register using the binary encoding of integers. 
General purpose quantum state preparation methods
enable the exact preparation of any 
 $q$-qubit state  by a quantum circuit composed of $O(2^q)$ CNOTs and single-qubit gates,
 see e.g.~\cite{grover2002creating,mottonen2004transformation}. 
  We conclude that the cost of preparing the normalized version of $\phi_{out}(x)$
 on a quantum computer scales linearly with $q^{-1}\|x\|_\lambda^2$ and $\log{(1/\epsilon)}$.
 The state $\phi_{out}(x)$ is expressed using $Q_{out}=q|\mathrm{supp}(x)|\le sq = O(q)$ qubits. 
For simplicity, let us assume that all multi-indices in the support of $\phi_{out}(x)$ belong to $\calJ_1$
(this assumption is not essential and can be removed simply by performing the projection of $\phi_{out}(x)$ onto $\calJ_1$).
Changing the binary encoding of  multi-indices 
to the encoding by $Q$-bit strings described earlier, one can consider the  readout state
$\phi_{out}(x)$ as a $Q$-qubit state.



\subsection{Putting everything together}
\label{sec:runtime}

Define our approximation of $v(t,x)=\la \psi_{out}(x)|\psi(t)\ra$ as 
\be
v_1(t,x) = \la \phi_{out}(x) \otimes 0_{anc} | U_\ell|\phi(0) \otimes 0_{anc}\ra,
\ee
where $|0_{anc}\ra = |00\ldots0\ra$ is the state of all ancillary qubits,
 $U_\ell$ is a unitary quantum circuit satisfying Eq.~(\ref{U_ell}),
 and $|\phi_{out}(x)\ra$ is our approximation of the readout state.
We shall choose the regularization scheme parameters $r,R$ and the number of Trotter steps $\ell$ such that 
\be
\label{error_v1}
|v(t,x)-v_1(t,x)|\le \frac{\epsilon}{2},
\ee
where $\epsilon$ is the desired error tolerance of Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm}.
Define normalized states
\be
|\hat{\phi}(0)\ra = \frac1{\|\phi(0)\|} |\phi(0)\ra \quad \mbox{and} \quad |\hat{\phi}_{out}(x)\ra = \frac1{\|\phi_{out}(x)\|} |\phi_{out}(x)\ra.
\ee
 The inner product 
\be
v_2(t,x) = \la \hat{\phi}_{out}(x) \otimes 0_{anc} | U_\ell|\hat{\phi}(0) \otimes 0_{anc}\ra
\ee
can be estimated using the standard Hadamard test since we have already showed
how to prepare the states $|\hat{\phi}(0)\ra$ and $|\hat{\phi}_{out}(x)\ra$.  We have
\be
v_1(t,x) = \| \phi_{out}(x)\| \cdot \|\phi(0)\| \cdot v_2(t,x).
\ee
As discussed above, the norms $\|\phi(0)\|$ and $\|\phi_{out}(x)\|$ admit a simple analytic formula.
Thus it suffices to estimate $v_2(t,x)$ with an error 
\be
\epsilon_{v_2} = \frac{\epsilon}{2 \|\phi(0)\| \cdot \|\phi_{out}(x)\|} \ge   \frac{\epsilon}{2 \|\phi(0)\| \cdot \|\psi_{out}(x)\|}
\ge \epsilon \cdot  poly(q^{-1} \lambda_1) \cdot \exp{\left[ -q^{-1} \| x\|_\lambda^2\right]}.
\ee
Here we used Eqs.~(\ref{phi(0)_norm},\ref{readout_state_norm}).
The number of circuit repetitions required for the Hadamard test scales as $O(1/\epsilon_{v_2}^2)$
and the output of the Hadamard test approximates $v(t,x)$ with the desired error $\epsilon$.

It remains to choose the regularization parameters $r,R$ and the number of Trotter steps $\ell$ to satisfy Eq.~(\ref{error_v1}).
By the triangle inequality,
\be
|v(t,x)-v_1(t,x)|\le \epsilon_{reg} + \epsilon_{tro} + \epsilon_{sim} +  \epsilon_{out},
\ee
where the four terms represent the regularization error,  Trotter error,  Hamiltonian simulation error,
and  the readout error:
\be
\epsilon_{reg} = \|\psi_{out}(x)\| \cdot \| \psi(t) - \phi(t)\|,
\ee
\be
\epsilon_{tro} =  \|\psi_{out}(x)\| \cdot \| \phi(t) - \calS(t/\ell)^\ell \phi(0)\|,
\ee
\be
\epsilon_{sim} = \|\psi_{out}(x)\| \cdot   \|  \calS(t/\ell)^\ell  \phi(0) - \la 0_{anc} | U_\ell |\phi(0)\otimes 0_{anc}\ra \|,
\ee
\be
\epsilon_{out} = \| \phi(0)\|\cdot \| \psi_{out}(x)-\phi_{out}(x)\|.
\ee
One can satisfy Eq.~(\ref{error_v1}) whenever all errors $\epsilon_{reg}, \epsilon_{tro},\epsilon_{sim},\epsilon_{out}$
are at most $\epsilon/8$.
From Theorem~\ref{thm:regul} one gets $\epsilon_{reg}\le \epsilon/8$ by choosing 
\be
\label{choice_of_r}
r= O(\gamma^2 \epsilon^{-2} \| \phi(0)\|^2 \cdot  \exp{\left[ 2q^{-1} \| x\|_\lambda^2\right]}) =J^2 q^{-1} \epsilon^{-2} \cdot poly(q/\lambda_1)
\cdot  \exp{\left[ 2q^{-1} \| x\|_\lambda^2\right]}.
\ee
Here we used Eqs.~(\ref{gamma_from_J},\ref{phi(0)_norm},\ref{readout_state_norm}).
Once $r$ is specified according to Eq.~(\ref{choice_of_r}), the second regularization parameter $R$ is upper bounded as $R\le r(\lambda_N/\lambda_1)$
and the maximum order of Hermite polynomials $K=\max_{\mm \in \calJ_1} |\mm|$ is upper bounded as $K\le r/\lambda_1$,
see Section~\ref{sec:regul-sz}.
Next   use Eq.~(\ref{Trotter_error_final}) to choose the number of Trotter steps $\ell$ such that 
$\epsilon_{tro}\le \epsilon/8$. Clearly, $\ell$ scales polynomially with $t,\epsilon^{-1},J,J_1,\lambda_N,\lambda_1^{-1}$,
and  $\exp{\left[ 2q^{-1} \| x\|_\lambda^2\right]}$.
Setting $\epsilon_{sim}=\epsilon/8$ determines the gate complexity of the Hamiltonian simulation circuit $U_\ell$.
As commented in the end of Section~\ref{sec:time_evolution}, the gate complexity scales polynomially with
$\ell,t,\epsilon_{sim}^{-1},\log{(N)}, K,\lambda_N,q^{-1},J_1,R$. For the  chosen $\ell, K, R$, the gate
complexity of $U_\ell$ scales polynomially with $t,\epsilon^{-1},J,J_1,\lambda_N,\lambda_1^{-1}$,
and  $\exp{\left[ q^{-1} \| x\|_\lambda^2\right]}$.
Finally, the gate complexity of preparing the readout state 
scales linearly with $q^{-1}\|x\|_\lambda^2$ and $\log{(1/\epsilon_{out})}$, see Section~\ref{sec:readout_state_prep}.
This complexity is linear in $q^{-1}\|x\|_\lambda^2$ and logarithmic in $1/\|\phi(0)\| = poly(q^{-1} \lambda_1)$.

To conclude, our quantum algorithm  has gate and qubit count scaling
polynomially with  $\log{(N)}$,  $t$, $\epsilon^{-1}$, $J$, $J_1$, $\lambda_1^{-1}$, $\lambda_N$,
and exponentially with $q^{-1} \| x\|_\lambda^2$, as claimed in Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm}.



\section{Examples of divergence-free systems}
\label{sec:examples}

In this section we describe explicit examples of nonlinear dynamical systems that can be simulated using our quantum algorithm: a nonlinear oscillator and 2D \ac{nse} equation describing a flow of an incompressible fluid. We also benchmark our quantum algorithm by performing numerical experiments. 


\subsection{Nonlinear oscillator.} 
\label{sec:pendulum}

Suppose $N=2$. Consider a nonlinear oscillator
\be
\label{pendulum1}
\frac{dX_1}{dt}   = -\lambda X_1 + \omega(\|X\|) X_2 \quad \mbox{and} \quad 
\frac{dX_2}{dt}   = -\lambda X_2 - \omega(\|X\|) X_1.
\ee
where $\|X\|=\sqrt{X_1^2+X_2^2}$ and $\omega$ is an arbitrary  function.
Let us check that the system Eq.~(\ref{pendulum1}) is divergence-free.
We have $c_1(x) = x_2 \omega(\|x\|)$ and $c_2(x)=-x_1 \omega(\|x\|)$. Thus
\be
\sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\partial c_i(x)}{\partial x_i} = \frac{x_1 x_2}{\|x\|} \left. \frac{d \omega(r)}{dr}\right|_{r=\|x\|} -  \frac{x_1 x_2}{\|x\|} \left. \frac{d \omega(r)}{dr}\right|_{r=\|x\|} = 0
\ee
and $\sum_{i=1}^N \lambda_i x_i c_i(x) = \lambda (x_1 x_2 - x_1 x_2)\omega(\|x\|)=0$.
The system has nonlinear strength
\be
J = \sup_{x\in \RR^2} \sqrt{ c_1^2(x) + c_2^2(x)} =  \sup_{r\ge 0} r \cdot \omega(r).
\ee
For numerical experiments we choose
\be
\label{omega}
\omega(r) = 1+ r^2,
\ee
so that $J=\infty$.
Then
\be
c_1(x) = x_2 ( 1+  x_1^2 + x_2^2) \quad \mbox{and} \quad c_2(x) = -x_1(1+  x_1^2 + x_2^2).
\ee
Let
\be
\eta = \frac{q}{2\lambda}.
\ee
Then   the operators $\Lambda$ and $C$ in the Kolmogorov equation have the form
\be
\Lambda = \lambda (a_1^\dag a_1 + a_2^\dag a_2)
\ee
and
\be
C = C_1 + C_2,
\ee
\be
C_1 = (a_2+a_2^\dag) \left( I + \eta  (a_1+ a_1^\dag)^2    + \eta  (a_2 + a_2^\dag)^2 \right) a_1,
\ee
\be
C_2 = -(a_1+a_1^\dag) \left( I + \eta (a_1+a_1^\dag)^2 + \eta  (a_2+a_2^\dag)^2\right) a_2.
\ee
Here $C_i$ is the second-quantized representation of the operator $\calC_i = c_i(x) \partial/\partial x_i$
obtained by replacing $\partial/\partial x_i$ with $a_i (2\lambda/q)^{1/2}$ and replacing
$x_i$ with $(2\lambda/q)^{-1/2}(a_i + a_i^\dag)$, as defined in  Section~\ref{sec:chaos}.
After simple algebra this gives
\be
C =\left( I + \eta (a_1 + a_1^\dag)^2 +   \eta (a_2 + a_2^\dag)^2 \right)  \left( a_2^\dag a_1 - a_1^\dag a_2\right).
\ee


