\begin{abstract}
Query optimizer is a crucial module for database management systems.
% to determine efficient query plans. 
Existing optimizers exhibit two flawed paradigms:
(1) cost-based optimizers
%@qz
%(CBOs) 
use dynamic programming with \emph{cost} models but face search space explosion and heuristic pruning constraints;
(2) value-based ones
%@qz
%(VBOs) 
train \emph{value} networks to enable efficient beam search, but incur higher training costs and lower accuracy.
They also lack mechanisms to detect queries where they may perform poorly. 
To determine more efficient plans, we propose \emph{Delta}, a mixed cost-based query optimization framework that consists of a compatible query detector and a two-stage planner.
Delta first employs a Mahalanobis distance-based detector to preemptively filter out incompatible queries where the planner might perform poorly. 
% This safety mechanism ensures robust execution by routing high-risk queries to fallback optimizers. 
For compatible queries, Delta activates its two-stage mixed cost-based planner.
Stage $\Rmnum{1}$ serves as a \emph{coarse}-grained filter to generate high-quality candidate plans based on the value network via beam search, relaxing precision requirements and narrowing the search space.
Stage $\Rmnum{2}$ employs a \emph{fine}-grained ranker to determine the best plan from the candidate plans based on a learned \emph{cost} model.
% compensating for potential inaccuracies in stage $\Rmnum{1}$.
Moreover, to reduce training costs, we reuse and augment the training data from stage $\Rmnum{1}$ to train the model in stage $\Rmnum{2}$.
% Extensive experiments on four workloads demonstrate the remarkably superior effectiveness of \ours.
% Experimental results on three workloads demonstrate that \ours identifies higher-quality plans, achieving an average [X]\% faster execution time than PostgreSQL and outperforming the state-of-the-art learned methods by [Y]\%.
Experimental results on three workloads demonstrate that Delta identifies higher-quality plans, achieving an average 2.34$\times$ speedup over PostgreSQL and outperforming the state-of-the-art learned methods by 2.21$\times$.
\end{abstract}

