\begin{abstract}
  % Large language models (LLMs) excel in reasoning tasks like software programming via reinforcement learning (RL), relying on datasets with verification frameworks. However, hardware description languages (HDLs) like Verilog remain underexplored due to scarce high-quality data with testbench verification, a critical gap compared to software domains with unit-test-integrated datasets. To address this, we introduce CodeV-R1, a framework featuring two core innovations: 1) a rigorous Verilog verification tool using massive input-output pairs and hardware synthesis tools for functional checks, and 2) a data selection pipeline filtering challenging tasks solvable by advanced models but non-trivial for smaller ones. Trained via two stages—distilling DeepSeek-R1 into a base model and applying RL with our adaptive DAPO algorithm—CodeV-R1 achieves state-of-the-art results on VerilogEval and RTLLM benchmarks among Verilog-domain LLMs. Especially, we surpass Deepseek-r1 by 8.1\% and prior Verilog-domain SOTA by 20.7\% on RTLLM-v1.1. Our work bridges the verification and data quality divide in hardware LLMs, enabling robust RL-driven Verilog code generation.
  Large language models (LLMs) trained via reinforcement learning with verifiable reward (RLVR) have achieved breakthroughs on tasks with explicit, automatable verification, such as software programming and mathematical problems. Extending RLVR to electronic design automation (EDA), especially automatically generating hardware description languages (HDLs) like Verilog from natural-language (NL) specifications, however, poses three key challenges: the lack of automated and accurate verification environments, the scarcity of high‐quality NL–code pairs, and the prohibitive computation cost of RLVR. To this end, we introduce \xname, an RLVR framework for training Verilog generation LLMs. First, we develop a rule-based testbench generator that performs robust equivalence checking against golden references. Second, we propose a round-trip data synthesis method that pairs open-source Verilog snippets with LLM‐generated NL descriptions, verifies code–NL–code consistency via the generated testbench, and filters out inequivalent examples to yield a high-quality dataset. Third, we employ a two-stage ``distill-then-RL'' training pipeline: distillation for the cold start of reasoning abilities, followed by adaptive DAPO, our novel RLVR algorithm that can reduce training cost by adaptively adjusting sampling rate. The resulting model, \modelFinal, achieves 68.6 \% and 72.9 \% pass@1 on VerilogEval v2 and RTLLM v1.1, respectively, surpassing prior state-of-the-art by 12$\sim$20 \%, while matching or even exceeding the performance of 671B DeepSeek-R1. We will release our model, training pipeline, and dataset to facilitate research in EDA and LLM communities.
  % 
\end{abstract}


